consiSt("pr",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Tribunal Supremo de Puerto Rico (Supreme Court)","d":"Court of last resort. Also controls admission to the bar, courtesy (pro hac vice) admission, notaries, and lawyer discipline, and answers certified questions of Puerto Rico law from federal and state courts."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Tribunal de Apelaciones (Court of Appeals)","d":"Sits in San Juan in panels. Hears appeals of right from final trial judgments, discretionary certiorari from trial-court orders, and petitions to review final agency decisions."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Tribunal de Primera Instancia, Superior Division (Sección Superior)","d":"The single, island-wide general-jurisdiction trial court for civil and criminal cases, organized by judicial region."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Tribunal de Primera Instancia, Municipal Division (Sección Municipal)","d":"Part of the same trial court. Municipal judges handle limited matters assigned by law, such as smaller civil claims, warrants, and initial probable-cause determinations."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Status not confirmed from the collected texts","d":"The collected Puerto Rico court texts (civil and criminal procedure, evidence, court rules, ethics rules, e-filing guidelines) contain no anti-SLAPP procedure: no special motion to dismiss, no discovery stay, and no fee-shifting rule tied to speech or petitioning. An anti-SLAPP law, if one exists, would be a statute outside these rules. Before telling a client anything, search the Leyes de Puerto Rico Anotadas (LPRA) and the Oficina de Gerencia y Presupuesto virtual library (bvirtualogp.pr.gov) for any enacted law on strategic lawsuits against public participation, and confirm its number, effective date, and procedure. No statute is named here because none could be verified.","c":"None verified; check LPRA and enacted laws (Leyes) for any anti-SLAPP statute","v":false}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice is called ‘admisión por cortesía’ and goes to the Supreme Court","d":"A lawyer admitted in any U.S. jurisdiction may ask the Tribunal Supremo (not the trial judge) for courtesy admission to appear in a specific case before the courts, administrative agencies, or ADR proceedings, including arbitration. File the official Solicitud de Admisión por Cortesía form with the Supreme Court Clerk (Secretaría).\nThe Clerk grants a properly filed application unless the volume suggests regular practice in Puerto Rico. Filing 6 or more applications in one year, or applying while already admitted in 5 active cases, triggers review under listed factors: shortage of local specialists, complexity, a long client relationship, questions of the home jurisdiction’s law, discovery needs there, and the client’s welfare.\nThe Court may revoke a courtesy admission for cause.","c":"Reglamento TS R. 12(f)(1), (7), (8)","v":true},{"t":"What the application must include","d":"Certificate of good standing from the highest court of every U.S. jurisdiction where the applicant is admitted, issued within 90 days before filing. The applicant must not be disbarred or suspended anywhere.\nDisclosures: any disciplinary proceeding opened in the last 5 years; any discipline or suspension ever; any courtesy admission denied by a U.S. jurisdiction in the last 5 years (with reasons); and every Puerto Rico courtesy application in the last 2 years.\nThe applicant certifies familiarity with Puerto Rico’s procedural and ethics rules. Both signing lawyers certify the information is true.\nFee: $800 in internal revenue stamps (sellos de rentas internas) per case, and per case in consolidated matters. Not refunded on denial. The Court may waive it for pro bono or good cause; listed legal-aid and law-school clinic endorsements are exempt.","c":"Reglamento TS R. 12(f)(2)","v":true},{"t":"Local counsel: the endorsing Puerto Rico lawyer does real work","d":"A lawyer admitted by the Puerto Rico Supreme Court, in good standing there, must endorse and co-sign the application. That lawyer must:\n(A) appear as counsel of record with the applicant;\n(B) sign every pleading, motion, and document in the case;\n(C) receive service of everything served on the applicant; and\n(D) accompany the applicant at every appearance, deposition, or other proceeding unless the forum excuses it.\nOnce admitted, the applicant is automatically subject to the Puerto Rico Supreme Court’s disciplinary jurisdiction and must keep the application information current. Both lawyers answer to the client and the forum.","c":"Reglamento TS R. 12(f)(2)-(3)","v":true},{"t":"Courtesy admittees cannot e-file","d":"Lawyers admitted by courtesy cannot file through SUMAC (the mandatory e-filing system) until the judiciary implements the technology. They must file on paper at the Clerk’s office of the court with the case or in the courthouse drop box (buzón de presentaciones), during regular hours (8:30 a.m. to 5:00 p.m.). In practice the endorsing Puerto Rico lawyer, who must sign everything anyway, is the one with SUMAC access.","c":"Directrices SUMAC §§ V(3), VI(1)(d), XVI(4)","v":true},{"t":"Work that does not need courtesy admission","d":"No courtesy admission is needed only to: assist proceedings in a jurisdiction where the lawyer is admitted; consult with a Puerto Rico lawyer about that lawyer’s client’s case; consult, at the person’s request, with someone in Puerto Rico about hiring the lawyer; prepare a Puerto Rico case while reasonably believing courtesy admission will be granted; prepare a case to be filed elsewhere; or advise a Puerto Rico client from outside Puerto Rico. None of this permits holding out as admitted in Puerto Rico.\nThe new ethics rules add a remote-work safe harbor: a lawyer admitted elsewhere who is physically in Puerto Rico may work remotely, limited to the services the home jurisdiction authorizes, without claiming to be a Puerto Rico lawyer.","c":"Reglamento TS R. 12(f)(5)-(6); R. Conducta Prof. 5.5(c), (f)","v":true},{"t":"Language of filings and proceedings","d":"Civil pleadings, motions, and applications may be filed in Spanish or English. Translating English filings is not required, but the court must order a translation when justice requires it, when it is indispensable to decide the case fairly, or when any party asks.\nThe rules otherwise assume Spanish as the working language: a witness who does not know Spanish testifies through a sworn interpreter, and the Supreme Court distributes its opinions in Spanish. Plan on Spanish-speaking co-counsel for hearings, depositions, and reading orders.","c":"R. Proc. Civ. 8.7; R. Evid. 614; Reglamento TS R. 9(l)","v":true},{"t":"Bar admission: no admission on motion","d":"Every applicant must pass Puerto Rico’s own bar exam (Reválida General); there is no reciprocity route in the collected rules. Requirements: at least 21 years old; a law degree from a school approved by the ABA or the Puerto Rico Supreme Court; a bachelor’s degree before law school (for U.S. and Puerto Rico graduates); good reputation; and passing the exam.\nThe exam is written in Spanish and may be answered in Spanish or English. It is given twice a year. After passing, the applicant goes before the Comisión de Reputación and takes the oath before the Supreme Court.","c":"Reglamento TS R. 12(a)-(d); Reglamento para la Admisión de Aspirantes R. 4.1.1, 5.2.1-5.2.2 (revised Oct. 2022)","v":true},{"t":"Continuing legal education","d":"Active Puerto Rico lawyers need 24 CLE hours every 3 years, at least 4 in professional ethics. Active notaries must take at least 9 of the 24 in notarial law. Lawyers admitted only by courtesy are outside the CLE program’s definition of covered professionals.\nSeparately, a one-time 6-hour course on the new Rules of Professional Conduct is due by December 31, 2026 (see Ethics).\nCurrency note: the CLE Reglamento text is current only to February 2024 and does not reflect the 2025 ethics-rules CLE resolutions.","c":"Reglamento PEJC R. 5(Q), 29(A); Res. ER-2025-02","v":true},{"t":"Notary practice is separate","d":"Puerto Rico notaries are lawyers with a separate license. A notary must be admitted to the Puerto Rico bar and also pass a separate notarial exam (Reválida Notarial), then apply to the Supreme Court Clerk with the bond required by law. No one may act as a notary in Puerto Rico without Supreme Court authorization, and courtesy admission authorizes appearing as a lawyer in one case only.","c":"Reglamento TS R. 12(f)(1), 13(a); Reglamento para la Admisión de Aspirantes R. 4.3.1; R. Conducta Prof. 5.5(g)","v":true},{"t":"Domesticating an out-of-state judgment (exequátur)","d":"There is no simple registration procedure in the civil rules. Recognition goes through exequátur in the Tribunal de Primera Instancia, either by complaint against everyone affected by the judgment or by a sworn ex parte petition signed by all of them.\nAttach a certified, complete copy of the judgment. A Spanish translation is required only if the judgment is in neither Spanish nor English.\nFor a judgment from a U.S. state or territory, the court checks that the rendering court had personal and subject-matter jurisdiction, observed due process, and that the judgment was not obtained by fraud. Some cases also require notice to public officials (the Family Affairs Prosecutor where minors’ or incapacitated persons’ interests are involved, the prosecutor when the judgment is to be recorded in the Property Registry, or the Secretary of Justice in matters of high public interest). Once recognized, it is enforced like a Puerto Rico judgment.","c":"R. Proc. Civ. 55.1-55.6","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Reglas de Conducta Profesional (effective January 1, 2026)","d":"The Supreme Court adopted the Rules of Professional Conduct of Puerto Rico by Resolution ER-2025-02 (June 17, 2025), effective January 1, 2026. They use the ABA Model Rules as the starting point, with exceptions, and for the first time govern lawyers and notaries in one body of rules. Each rule has a comment that explains it but adds no obligations.\nThe Spanish text is the adopted version. The Supreme Court’s Bureau of Translations publishes an English translation. Rely on the Spanish text where the two differ.","c":"Res. ER-2025-02 (June 17, 2025); R. Conducta Prof. 1.0","v":true},{"t":"What it replaced, and which code governs older conduct","d":"The new rules replace the Código de Ética Profesional of 1970, which was based mainly on the ABA’s 1908 Canons. The Cánones were repealed as of January 1, 2026, but disciplinary proceedings for violations before that date are still governed by the 1970 Cánones. For conduct spanning both periods, check which code applies to each act.\nNot yet in force: Rule 7.2(b)(2) on lawyer referral services takes effect only after the Court approves qualifying criteria.\nCross-reference lag: Civil Procedure Rule 9.2 (withdrawal of counsel) still tells lawyers to comply with the ‘cánones del Código de Ética Profesional.’ Read it as pointing to the new rules for post-2025 conduct, but confirm.","c":"Res. ER-2025-02, at 6; 4 LPRA Ap. IX (1970 Code, historical); R. Proc. Civ. 9.2","v":true},{"t":"Mandatory 6-hour CLE on the new rules, due December 31, 2026","d":"Every lawyer and notary authorized by the Supreme Court, whether active or temporarily suspended, must complete at least 6 CLE hours mainly on the new rules by December 31, 2026, in courses approved by the CLE Program (PEJC). It is a one-time requirement. The hours count toward the 24-hour triennial minimum and the 4 ethics hours, but must be completed even if the ethics hours are already done. Report compliance to the PEJC with the certification form and course certificate.\nExcluded: Puerto Rico and federal judges in Puerto Rico; inactive or voluntarily withdrawn lawyers; lawyers permanently out of practice; those with a good-cause exemption; Advisory Committee members; and lawyers already admitted by courtesy on June 17, 2025. The Court may require new courtesy applicants to take hours on the new rules.\nEnforcement: 75 days after the deadline the PEJC refers non-compliant lawyers to the Court. From January 1, 2027, the Court will not consider any reinstatement or readmission application without proof of the 6 hours.","c":"Res. ER-2025-02, at 6-8; Res. ER-2025-02A, 2025 TSPR 108 (Oct. 27, 2025)","v":true},{"t":"Difference from ABA Model Rules: nonlawyer ownership up to 49%","d":"Unlike ABA Model Rule 5.4, Puerto Rico lets a lawyer practice in a firm partly owned by nonlawyers, if all conditions are met: nonlawyer ownership does not exceed 49%; the nonlawyer contributes only money and provides no services (not even marketing); a Puerto Rico lawyer runs the firm, represents the nonlawyer owner on votes, notifies the Supreme Court when the arrangement begins, and files a sworn annual statement with the Clerk every January 15; there is no interference with professional judgment; confidentiality under Rule 1.6 is protected; clients are told about the nonlawyer owner; and the firm meets its pro bono duty. The Court will evaluate the rule within 3 years after it takes effect.","c":"R. Conducta Prof. 5.4(a)(5), (b)(1)-(8), (d)","v":true},{"t":"Difference from ABA Model Rules: standalone technology and AI rule","d":"Rule 1.19 is a separate rule on technological competence and diligence, covering both lawyers and notaries. The adopting resolution says the Court asked for it to cover technology, including artificial intelligence, and lists it among the rules that depart from the ABA Model Rules’ structure. The comment says diligence can be breached by failing to check an electronic notice, filing documents without validating content generated by technological tools, or delegating tasks to such tools without proper oversight.","c":"R. Conducta Prof. 1.19 & cmts. [1]-[2]; Res. ER-2025-02, at 2-3 & n.2","v":true},{"t":"Other notable changes for practitioners","d":"Contingent fee agreements must be in writing and signed by the client, with a written closing statement at the end. The adopting resolution calls this a substantial change from prior Puerto Rico law. Contingent fees remain barred in divorce, support, and community-property division matters and in criminal defense.\nThe new rules drop the 1970 Code’s general ‘appearance of impropriety’ standard (Canon 38) as a basis for discipline and list specific kinds of misconduct instead.\nRemote work: Rule 5.5(f) expressly lets a lawyer admitted elsewhere work remotely while physically in Puerto Rico.","c":"R. Conducta Prof. 1.5(c)-(d), 5.5(f), 8.4; Res. ER-2025-02, at 3, 6","v":true}],"style":"Commonwealth of Puerto Rico","home":"https://poderjudicial.pr/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Reconsideration: 15 days, two different clocks, and simultaneous service","d":"From an interlocutory order or resolution: 15 days from notice, a strict-compliance term. From a judgment: 15 days from archivo en autos of the notice, a jurisdictional term that cannot be extended. The motion must be served on all parties within the same 15 days, and it must state with particularity the facts and law to reconsider. A motion that lacks that specificity is denied and does not toll the time to appeal. A proper motion tolls the appeal time for all parties.","c":"R. Proc. Civ. 47, 52.2(e)(2), 68.2"},{"t":"Appeal and certiorari: 30 days, and some cannot be saved","d":"Civil appeal to the Court of Appeals: 30 days from archivo en autos of the notice of judgment, jurisdictional. Certiorari from interlocutory trial orders: 30 days from notice, strict compliance, extendable only for special circumstances explained in the petition itself. Certiorari to the Supreme Court from Court of Appeals judgments in appeals: 30 days, jurisdictional. The trial court cannot extend the terms in Rules 43.1, 44.1, 47, 48.2, 48.4, 49.2, or 52.2.","c":"R. Proc. Civ. 52.2(a)-(b), 68.2; Reglamento TA R. 13(A), 32(B)-(C)"},{"t":"Court of Appeals: the fee payment date is the filing date","d":"Uploading the appeal to SUMAC is not filing. The filing receipt warns that the document is not officially filed until the filing fees are paid, and the filing date is the fee payment date. It does not relate back to the upload. The 11:59 p.m. e-filing rule also does not apply to filings that require a fee. Pay on the platform the same day. If the trial case has no electronic record, also send the trial court clerk a stamped cover page within 72 hours (strict compliance).","c":"Reglamento TA R. 14(A); Directrices SUMAC §§ VII, XVI(1)"},{"t":"Serve the appellate brief on the other side within the filing period","d":"The appellant must serve the appeal and appendices on the other parties within the time to file the appeal (strict compliance) and certify the date of service. E-filing serves lawyers, but pro se parties and others the law requires must be served separately.","c":"Reglamento TA R. 13(B)"},{"t":"Summons must be served within 120 days, or the case is dismissed","d":"Serve the summons within 120 days of filing the complaint (or of issuance of a summons by publication). If service is not completed in time, the court must dismiss without prejudice. A second dismissal for the same failure operates as an adjudication on the merits. Service cannot be done through SUMAC. Out-of-island defendants may be served personally, under the law of the place of service, by letters rogatory, by publication, or as the court orders.","c":"R. Proc. Civ. 4.3(b)-(c), 67.6"},{"t":"Nonresident plaintiffs must post a cost bond","d":"When the claimant lives outside Puerto Rico or is a foreign corporation, the court must require a bond of at least $1,000 for costs, expenses, and attorney’s fees, and all proceedings are stayed until it is posted. If it is not posted within 60 days of the order, the court dismisses the case. Narrow exceptions apply: indigent litigants exempt from filing fees, co-owners suing over Puerto Rico property alongside a resident co-owner, and partition of property located in Puerto Rico.","c":"R. Proc. Civ. 69.5"},{"t":"Court of Appeals reconsideration: 15 days, no extensions","d":"A motion to reconsider a Court of Appeals decision, civil or criminal, is due within 15 days of archivo en autos of the notice, and the term cannot be extended. A timely motion stops the clock to go to the Supreme Court, which restarts when notice of the ruling on the motion is filed.","c":"Reglamento TA R. 84(A); R. Proc. Civ. 52.2(f)"},{"t":"Criminal appeals run from the date of sentencing","d":"A criminal appeal is due within 30 days after the sentence is pronounced, not after notice. The term is jurisdictional. A timely new-trial or reconsideration motion restarts the 30 days from notice of the order resolving it. Certiorari from a conviction on a guilty plea is also 30 days from the sentence, jurisdictional.","c":"Reglamento TA R. 23(A), 32(A)"},{"t":"Agency review: 30 days to the Court of Appeals","d":"Judicial review of a final agency order or decision goes directly to the Court of Appeals within 30 days (jurisdictional) of archivo en autos of the notice, or of the restarted date after a timely agency reconsideration motion, unless a special law sets a different term.","c":"Reglamento TA R. 57"},{"t":"Pro hac vice counsel cannot e-file and must bring local counsel everywhere","d":"A courtesy-admitted lawyer has no SUMAC filing access and must file on paper. The endorsing Puerto Rico lawyer must sign every filing and attend every hearing and deposition unless excused. Budget for full local-counsel participation, not a name on the signature block.","c":"Reglamento TS R. 12(f)(2); Directrices SUMAC § V(3)"},{"t":"Withdrawal needs leave of court","d":"Counsel who has appeared must move in writing to withdraw, give reasons, provide the client’s phone and mailing address, and certify the client was notified. The court may refuse in exceptional cases if a party’s rights would be seriously harmed or the case unduly delayed.","c":"R. Proc. Civ. 9.2"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory challenge; sworn recusal motion within 20 days","d":"Puerto Rico has no peremptory change of judge. Recusal is for cause under Civil Procedure Rule 63.1, which lists grounds from bias and financial interest to a catch-all for anything that could reasonably cast doubt on impartiality.\nProcedure: a sworn motion filed with the challenged judge within 20 days after the party learns of the ground, stating specific facts with supporting documents and affidavits. If these formalities are missing, the judge may keep going with the case.\nIf the judge agrees, the judge states the ground in writing and the case is reassigned. If not, the judge stops acting and sends the record to the administrative judge, who assigns another judge to decide the motion within 30 days.\nTrap: once a judge has begun acting in a case, a lawyer whose participation would require recusal cannot join the case.","c":"R. Proc. Civ. 63.1, 63.2(a)-(d)","v":true},{"t":"Recusal in the Court of Appeals","d":"A recusal motion must be written and sworn, state its facts, and be filed as soon as the grounds are known. If the challenged appellate judge declines to step aside, the administrative judge assigns the motion to a different panel for final decision.","c":"Reglamento TA R. 9(B)-(D)","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"P.R. Const. art. II, § 4 | Broader than federal: not established here","d":"Article II, § 4 of the Puerto Rico Constitution bars laws abridging freedom of speech or press, peaceable assembly, or petitioning the government. The collected texts do not include the Constitution or case law, so whether the Puerto Rico Supreme Court reads it more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked.","c":"P.R. Const. art. II, § 4 (not in collected texts)","v":false},{"t":"Practical effect: reputation and privacy are also constitutional","d":"Article II, § 8 gives every person a constitutional right to legal protection against abusive attacks on honor, reputation, and private or family life. Defamation and privacy claims in Puerto Rico are therefore framed against a constitutional interest on the plaintiff’s side as well. Not checked against the collected texts.","c":"P.R. Const. art. II, § 8 (not in collected texts)","v":false}],"language":[{"t":"In short","d":"Spanish is the working language of Puerto Rico courts, but civil filings may be in English without translation unless the court orders one."},{"t":"Civil filings: Spanish or English","d":"Pleadings, motions, and applications may be filed in Spanish or English. A party who knows neither may file in their own language with copies in Spanish or English. Translating English filings is not required, but the court must order translation when justice requires it, when it is indispensable to a fair decision, or when any party asks. A party opposing an English-speaking litigant can therefore force translations on request.","c":"R. Proc. Civ. 8.7","v":true},{"t":"Testimony and decisions in Spanish","d":"A witness who does not know Spanish testifies through an interpreter the judge finds qualified, sworn to translate faithfully. The Supreme Court distributes its opinions and judgments in Spanish. No collected rule requires certified English translations of exhibits generally, but interpreter and translation needs should be planned early.","c":"R. Evid. 614; Reglamento TS R. 9(l)","v":true},{"t":"Supreme Court certified questions","d":"Briefs in certified-question proceedings may be in English or Spanish without translation, but must be signed by a lawyer admitted by the Puerto Rico Supreme Court. The Court’s answering opinion is translated into English by its Bureau of Translations, with costs split equally among the parties unless the certifying court orders otherwise.","c":"Reglamento TS R. 25(h), (l)","v":true},{"t":"Foreign judgments","d":"For exequátur, a faithful Spanish translation is required only when the judgment is in neither Spanish nor English.","c":"R. Proc. Civ. 55.3(b)","v":true},{"t":"Bar exam","d":"The bar exam is written in Spanish and may be answered in Spanish or English.","c":"Reglamento para la Admisión de Aspirantes R. 5.2.1","v":true}],"other":[{"t":"SUMAC e-filing is mandatory for Puerto Rico lawyers","d":"Lawyers admitted in Puerto Rico and registered in the Registro Único de Abogados (RUA) must file through SUMAC (Sistema Unificado de Manejo y Administración de Casos), using their RUA credentials. Filing in SUMAC is filing with the court, and it also serves the other lawyers and self-represented parties. An electronic signature counts as a handwritten one.\nSummonses and subpoenas cannot be served electronically; serve them under Rules 4 and 40.\nHours: e-filing is open around the clock, every day, and anything filed by 11:59 p.m. counts as filed that day, except filings that require a filing fee. The 24-hour window does not change how deadlines are counted under Rule 68.\nThe Court of Appeals rules make SUMAC the required channel for all appellate filings, with narrow exceptions for pro se parties, items that cannot be filed electronically, and technical failures.","c":"R. Proc. Civ. 67.6; Directrices SUMAC §§ V(1), VI(1), XVI(1)-(3); Reglamento TA R. 2.1","v":true},{"t":"Interlocutory review is narrow","d":"The Court of Appeals may grant certiorari from an interlocutory trial order only for orders under Rules 56 (provisional remedies) and 57 (injunctions), or the denial of a dispositive motion. Exceptions: rulings on admitting essential fact or expert witnesses, evidentiary privileges, entries of default, family-relations cases, cases of public interest, or where waiting for appeal would be an irremediable failure of justice. The court need not explain a denial. Everything else waits for the appeal from the final judgment.","c":"R. Proc. Civ. 52.1","v":true},{"t":"Government parties double the clock","d":"When the Commonwealth, its municipalities, their officials, or instrumentalities (but not public corporations) are parties, the answer is due in 60 days instead of 30, and that period cannot be extended. The appeal to the Court of Appeals, and certiorari to the Supreme Court from its appellate judgments, are due in 60 days (jurisdictional) instead of 30. This applies to every party in the case, not only the government.","c":"R. Proc. Civ. 10.1, 52.2(c); Reglamento TA R. 13(A)","v":true},{"t":"Deadlines run from ‘archivo en autos’ of the notice","d":"Many post-judgment deadlines run from the date a copy of the notice of judgment is filed in the record (archivo en autos), not from receipt. For electronic notices, the date the platform sends the notice is the archivo date. If notice was mailed and the mailing date differs from the archivo date, the term runs from the mailing date. The extra 3 days for mail service do not apply to terms counted from archivo en autos of the notice of judgment.","c":"R. Proc. Civ. 52.2(c) (last para.), 68.3; Reglamento TA R. 13(A)","v":true},{"t":"Criminal procedure text is out of date","d":"The official compilation of the 1963 Rules of Criminal Procedure includes amendments only through February 2020. It still shows the pre-2022 text of Rule 10 (‘delitos graves de cuarto grado’), which Act 22-2022 amended to conform to the 2012 Penal Code. Verify any criminal rule against laws enacted after February 2020 before relying on it.","c":"R. Proc. Crim. 10 (34 LPRA Ap. II); Ley 22-2022","v":true},{"t":"Complex civil cases have their own rules","d":"Puerto Rico has separate Rules for Complex Civil Litigation Cases, which apply once a case is designated complex. The collected copy is certified current only to May 2016, so confirm the current text.","c":"Reglas para Casos Civiles de Litigación Compleja (ER-99-7, as amended by ER-2006-2)","v":false}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Reglamento del Tribunal de Apelaciones, 4 LPRA Ap. XXII-B (Resolución ER-2004-0010, as amended through Resolución ER-2025-0003 of December 19, 2025; restated by Resolución ER-2025-0001, approved April 24, 2025, 215 DPR 943, in force June 16, 2025), including its “Comentario – Enmiendas del 2025” notes. Reglamento del Tribunal Supremo de Puerto Rico, 4 LPRA Ap. XXI-B (ER-2011-05, as amended through ER-2023-0001 of June 14, 2023). Reglas de Procedimiento Civil, 32 LPRA Ap. V (Secretariado compilation with amendments through March 2023), Rules 8.7, 47, 48, 52, 68. Reglas de Procedimiento Criminal, 34 LPRA Ap. II (Secretariado compilation current only through February 2020), Rules 193–196, 216–217. Directrices Administrativas para la Presentación y Notificación Electrónica de Documentos mediante el SUMAC (OAJP-2013-173, as amended through OAJP-2025-131 and Circular Núm. 26 of April 24, 2025). Official Spanish texts from poderjudicial.pr; English summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Puerto Rico: pick the right recurso, then watch whether the term is jurisdictional","d":"Puerto Rico has two appellate courts. The Tribunal de Apelaciones (TA) hears appeals (apelación) from final civil and criminal judgments of the Tribunal de Primera Instancia (TPI), discretionary certiorari against interlocutory orders and a few other rulings, judicial review (revisión) of final agency decisions, and some original writs. The Tribunal Supremo (TS) reviews the TA almost entirely by discretionary certiorari; a direct appeal to the TS exists only in narrow cases.\nMost terms are 30 days, but they come in two kinds. A jurisdictional term (término jurisdiccional) cannot be extended or excused by anyone. A strict-compliance term (término de cumplimiento estricto) can be excused only for good cause (justa causa) that you explain in the filing itself. Labeling the wrong recurso, or treating a jurisdictional term as flexible, is the classic way to lose an appeal.\nSince June 16, 2025, everything at the TA is filed through SUMAC, and a filing that carries a fee is not filed until the fee is paid. Read “What changed on June 16, 2025” below if you learned TA practice before then.","c":"Reglas 2.1, 13, 32, 57, 72, 83 Reglamento TA; Regla 52 R. Proc. Civ.; Regla 20 Reglamento TS"},"groups":[["Which recurso, which court",[{"t":"Apelación to the TA: final civil judgments","d":"A final judgment (sentencia) of the TPI in a civil case is reviewed by apelación. The escrito de apelación is the appellant’s brief; no separate brief or memorandum of authorities is allowed. Interlocutory rulings not reviewable by certiorari can be raised in the appeal from the judgment, subject to the harmless-error rule (Regla 50 R. Proc. Civ.).","c":"Regla 13 y 16(C)(2) Reglamento TA; Reglas 52.1 y 52.2(a) R. Proc. Civ."},{"t":"Certiorari to the TA: civil interlocutory orders, only on listed subjects","d":"The TA may issue certiorari against a TPI interlocutory order or resolution only when it involves Rule 56 (provisional remedies) or Rule 57 (injunction), or the denial of a dispositive motion. By exception, it may also review rulings on the admissibility of essential fact or expert witnesses, evidentiary privileges, entries of default (anotaciones de rebeldía), family-relations cases, cases of public interest, or any situation where waiting for the appeal would be an irremediable failure of justice. When it denies certiorari in these cases the TA need not give reasons. Even inside these categories, issuance is discretionary; Regla 40 Reglamento TA lists the factors (for example, whether the ruling is contrary to law, whether this is the right stage, and whether review would cause undue piecemeal litigation).","c":"Regla 52.1 R. Proc. Civ.; Reglas 32(C) y 40 Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Certiorari to the TA: other rulings that go by certiorari","d":"Also by certiorari: (1) a criminal conviction on a guilty plea, which cannot be appealed (Regla 193 R. Proc. Crim.; Regla 32(A) Reglamento TA); (2) final resolutions in non-contentious proceedings (jurisdicción voluntaria) (Regla 32(B)); (3) any other order reviewable by certiorari under law, which the 2025 text says expressly includes protection orders and a TPI judgment on a petition to review an arbitration award (Regla 32(C)). Criminal interlocutory rulings also go by certiorari under Regla 32(C).","c":"Regla 32 Reglamento TA; Regla 193 R. Proc. Crim.; Regla 52.2(b) R. Proc. Civ."},{"t":"Criminal apelación to the TA","d":"The accused may appeal a final judgment after trial. The escrito de apelación in a criminal case is a short notice (see page limits); the full brief (alegato) comes later, 30 days after the record (expediente) is sent up, unless the TA orders otherwise. A guilty-plea conviction goes by certiorari, not appeal. A self-represented appellant in prison files by handing the notice to the custodian within the term.","c":"Reglas 193–196 R. Proc. Crim.; Reglas 23, 26, 28(A) y 30.1 Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Revisión judicial to the TA: final agency decisions","d":"Final orders, resolutions and regulations of administrative agencies (adjudicative or quasi-legislative) are reviewed by recurso de revisión under Part VII of the Reglamento TA and the LPAU (Ley 38-2017). A simplified recurso de revisión especial (a court form, which may be handwritten) is available to a person acting pro se against a final decision denying welfare (beneficencia social) services or benefits.","c":"Reglas 56, 57, 59 y 67 Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Original writs at the TA: habeas corpus and mandamus","d":"File the petition with the TA Secretaría, not directly with an individual judge (allowed only in extreme urgency when the court and Secretaría are unavailable). Serve all parties under the Rules of Civil Procedure. For mandamus against a judge to perform a ministerial duty in a pending case, the judge is not served with summons; notify the judge, the other parties and the court where the case is pending under Regla 13(B). A party may ask a panel to review a single judge’s resolution within 10 days after its notice is filed (archivo en autos).","c":"Reglas 54 y 55 Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Electoral cases: a separate fast track","d":"TPI judgments reviewing State Elections Commission (CEE) decisions under Art. 13.3 of the Código Electoral de 2020 (Ley 58-2020) go to the TA by certiorari under Part V, with terms of 10 days, 48 hours or 24 hours depending on how close the election is. Review in the TS goes by certiorari under the TS electoral rule.","c":"Reglas 41, 42 y 53 Reglamento TA; Regla 22 Reglamento TS"},{"t":"To the Tribunal Supremo: certiorari is the normal route","d":"The TS reviews TA judgments and resolutions by certiorari, which it issues at its discretion. A direct apelación to the TS is available only where the TA judgment declares a law, joint or concurrent resolution, rule, agency regulation or municipal ordinance unconstitutional, or where there is a substantial conflict among prior TA decisions; if the TS finds neither, it may treat the appeal as a certiorari, which must then meet certiorari requirements. The TS may likewise treat a certiorari as an appeal. Criminal judgments of the TA are reviewable only by certiorari. Other TS routes: certification (certificación) of novel or high-interest questions, motions in aid of jurisdiction, and recursos gubernativos against Property Registrars.","c":"Reglas 17, 18, 20, 23, 27 y 28 Reglamento TS; Regla 52.2(b) y (d) R. Proc. Civ.; Regla 217 R. Proc. Crim."},{"t":"Orders in aid of jurisdiction (auxilio de jurisdicción)","d":"To stop something from happening while a recurso is pending, ask the TA for an order in aid of jurisdiction. File it in the TA Secretaría with the case caption, follow the form rules for motions, and notify the other parties and anyone the order would bind at the same moment you file (personally, by phone or by email). If you file it with the recurso, that simultaneous notice must include the recurso and its appendix. Since 2025, the TA may grant such an order even when the request has non-jurisdictional defects.","c":"Regla 79 Reglamento TA; Regla 28 Reglamento TS"},{"t":"Effect of filing: what is and is not stayed","d":"Filing a civil appeal stays the TPI proceedings on what is appealed (the TPI may go on with other matters). It does not stay an injunction, mandamus or order to do or desist, a support (alimentos) order, or a custody or visitation order, unless the TA orders it. Filing a certiorari stays nothing unless the TA orders it; issuing the writ stays the TPI proceedings unless the TA says otherwise.","c":"Reglas 18 y 35 Reglamento TA; Regla 52.3 R. Proc. Civ."}]],["Jurisdictional vs. strict-compliance terms (the classic trap)",[{"t":"Jurisdictional terms cannot be extended or excused","d":"The TA will not grant an extension of a jurisdictional term, and a recurso filed late must be dismissed for lack of jurisdiction. The TPI cannot extend the terms in Civil Rules 47 (reconsideration), 48.2 (new trial) or 52.2 (appeal and certiorari) either. Jurisdictional: civil appeals (30 or 60 days); criminal appeals (30); certiorari against a guilty-plea conviction (30); certiorari in jurisdicción voluntaria (30); administrative revisión (30); electoral certiorari (10 days or less); reconsideration of a TPI judgment (15); certiorari to the TS against a TA judgment in an appeal (30 or 60); reconsideration in the TS (10 business days).","c":"Reglas 13, 23, 32(A)–(B), 42, 57, 72(C) y 83(B)(1) Reglamento TA; Reglas 47, 52.2 y 68.2 R. Proc. Civ.; Reglas 20(a) y 45(b) Reglamento TS"},{"t":"Strict-compliance terms: excusable only for good cause, shown up front","d":"A strict-compliance term may be exceeded only when special circumstances are duly supported in the filing itself; the court may dismiss a recurso filed outside such a term without good cause. Put the explanation in the petition, not in a later motion. Strict-compliance: certiorari to the TA against interlocutory orders and the other Regla 32(C) rulings (30 days); certiorari to the TS against TA rulings in discretionary recursos (30); reconsideration of a TPI interlocutory order (15); serving the recurso on the other parties within the filing term; notices to the lower court within 48 or 72 hours; supplementary certification of service (48 hours).","c":"Regla 52.2(b) R. Proc. Civ.; Reglas 13(B)(1), 14, 15, 32(C) y 83(B)(2) Reglamento TA; Regla 20(b) Reglamento TS"},{"t":"Defects of form and service: the court should let you fix them","d":"The TA must read its notice and form requirements to minimize dismissals and, for good cause, give a reasonable chance to cure form or service defects that do not affect the parties’ rights. The 2025 text of Regla 83(C) adds that the TA loses jurisdiction only for failure to meet a jurisdictional or strict-compliance requirement. This forgiveness does not reach a late jurisdictional filing.","c":"Reglas 12.1 y 83(C) Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Premature recursos","d":"A recurso filed while a timely reconsideration motion is still pending in the TPI is premature: the appeal term has not started running again. The TA may take steps to make the later appeal cheaper, but expect to refile after the reconsideration is decided and notified, within the new term.","c":"Regla 83(E) Reglamento TA; Regla 47 R. Proc. Civ."}]],["When the clock starts and how to count",[{"t":"The trigger is the archivo en autos of the copy of the notice","d":"Civil appeal and certiorari terms run from the filing in the record (archivo en autos) of a copy of the notice of the judgment or order. In SUMAC cases, that date is the date the platform sent the notice. If notice went by post and the mailing date differs from the archivo en autos date, count from the mailing date. Civil Rule 47 and 52.2(b) speak of the “fecha de notificación” for interlocutory orders; Regla 32(C) of the Reglamento TA speaks of the archivo en autos; in practice count from the later of the two dates if they differ.","c":"Regla 13(A) y 32 Reglamento TA; Reglas 52.2(c) (último párrafo) y 68.3 R. Proc. Civ."},{"t":"Criminal judgments: from the day sentence is pronounced","d":"Criminal appeal and guilty-plea certiorari terms run from the date the sentence was dictated if the defendant was present in the courtroom. If not present, count from the mailing of the notice when that date differs from the archivo en autos.","c":"Reglas 193 y 194 R. Proc. Crim.; Reglas 23(A) y 32(A) Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Counting days","d":"Do not count the day of the triggering event. If the last day is a Saturday, Sunday or legal holiday, the term runs to the end of the next business day. For terms under 7 days, intermediate weekends and holidays are excluded. A half-day holiday counts as a full holiday. The 3 extra days for service by mail do not apply to terms that run from the archivo en autos of a judgment.","c":"Reglas 68.1 y 68.3 R. Proc. Civ."},{"t":"Deadline hour","d":"TA: an electronic filing is due by 11:59 p.m. on the last day, and SUMAC accepts filings every day, including weekends and holidays. A paper filing must be made at the Secretaría from 8:30 a.m. to 5:00 p.m., or in the after-hours drop box (buzón) from 5:00 p.m. to 11:59 p.m. on business days. The SUMAC hours do not change how terms are computed. TS: its Reglamento still says 5:00 p.m. unless the court adopts extended-hours mechanisms.","c":"Regla 72(A) Reglamento TA; Directrices SUMAC, Sec. XVI; Regla 48(a) Reglamento TS"},{"t":"Extensions: ask at least 3 business days ahead","d":"An extension request must reach the Secretaría at least 3 business days before the term expires. Any extension runs from the original due date unless the panel says otherwise. Late filings are not accepted unless the panel allows them, and jurisdictional terms are never extended. In the TS, extension motions need concrete facts; an attorney’s heavy workload is generally not good cause.","c":"Regla 72(B)–(C) Reglamento TA; Regla 48(b)–(d) Reglamento TS"}]],["Reconsideration and other motions that stop the clock",[{"t":"In the TPI (civil): Rule 47 reconsideration","d":"Against a judgment: 15 days from the archivo en autos of the notice of judgment (jurisdictional). Against an interlocutory order or resolution: 15 days from its notice (strict compliance). The motion must state the facts and law with particularity; one that does not is denied and does not interrupt the term. Serve it on the other parties within the same 15 days (strict compliance). A proper, timely motion interrupts the term to seek review for all parties, and the full term starts again from the archivo en autos of the notice of the resolution deciding it.","c":"Reglas 47 y 52.2(e)(2), (g), (h) R. Proc. Civ."},{"t":"Other civil motions that interrupt the appeal term","d":"A timely Rule 43.1 motion for initial or additional findings and a timely Rule 48 new-trial motion (generally 15 days from the archivo en autos of the notice of judgment) also interrupt the appeal term, which restarts from the archivo en autos of the notice of the order deciding them.","c":"Reglas 48.2 y 52.2(e)(1), (3) R. Proc. Civ."},{"t":"In the TPI (criminal)","d":"A motion to reconsider the sentence or conviction filed within 15 days (not extendable) after sentence interrupts the appeal or certiorari term, which restarts from notice of the ruling on it. A new-trial motion under Rules 188(e) and 192 filed within the 30-day appeal term lets the appeal be filed within 30 days after notice of the order denying it. The criminal rules compilation in Consi’s sources is current only to February 2020, so check for later amendments.","c":"Regla 194 R. Proc. Crim.; Regla 23(A) Reglamento TA"},{"t":"In the TA: 15 days, one motion","d":"A motion to reconsider any TA decision, civil or criminal, must be filed within 15 days (not extendable) from the archivo en autos of the notice of the decision. A timely motion suspends the term to go to the TS, which starts again from the archivo en autos of the notice of the resolution that finally decides it. Argue only the specific errors; the other side may oppose within 10 days. A party whose first motion was denied may not file another. Electoral cases follow Regla 42 (10 days, deemed denied if the TA does not rule within 5 days; no reconsideration within 30 days before an election).","c":"Regla 84 Reglamento TA; Regla 216 R. Proc. Crim.; Regla 52.2(e)(4) y (f) R. Proc. Civ."},{"t":"In the TS: 10 business days, jurisdictional","d":"A motion to reconsider a TS decision is due within 10 business days (jurisdictional), may not exceed 10 pages, and cannot be extended or supported by a separate memorandum. A second motion by the same party is allowed only within 3 business days after notice of the denial of the first; no further motions. In electoral certiorari the term is 3 days.","c":"Regla 45 Reglamento TS; Regla 22(f) Reglamento TS"},{"t":"Agency reconsideration","d":"The 30-day revisión term runs from the archivo en autos of the notice of the agency’s final order, or from the applicable date when a timely reconsideration motion interrupted it. The agency-level reconsideration terms are in the LPAU (Ley 38-2017), which is not among Consi’s sources for this page; check the statute.","c":"Regla 57 Reglamento TA"}]],["Government parties get 60 days (sometimes)",[{"t":"60 days to appeal when the government is a party: for every party","d":"When the Commonwealth (ELA), its officials, one of its instrumentalities other than a public corporation, or a municipality or its officials is a party, any aggrieved party in that suit, not only the government, has a 60-day jurisdictional term to appeal a TPI judgment to the TA.","c":"Regla 13(A) Reglamento TA; Regla 52.2(c) R. Proc. Civ."},{"t":"60 days in the TS too, but only for appeal-track cases","d":"The 60-day term also applies to certiorari to the TS against a TA judgment in an appeal and to a direct civil appeal to the TS in such cases.","c":"Regla 52.2(c) R. Proc. Civ.; Reglas 17(a) y 20(a)(1) Reglamento TS"},{"t":"Where the 60 days does not apply","d":"Public corporations are excluded. Civil Rule 52.2(c) covers only appeals and TS certiorari against TA judgments in appeals; certiorari to the TA against interlocutory orders stays at 30 days. Criminal terms and the 30-day administrative revisión term have no government extension in these sources (a special law may set a different term).","c":"Regla 52.2(b)–(c) R. Proc. Civ.; Reglas 23, 32 y 57 Reglamento TA"}]],["Filing through SUMAC",[{"t":"Electronic filing is mandatory at the TA","d":"All recursos and other filings go through SUMAC (the Sistema Unificado de Manejo y Administración de Casos), and filing in SUMAC equals filing in the Secretaría. Exceptions: self-represented litigants (until the technology is available), items that by nature, law or court order cannot be e-filed, and technical problems with the platform. Cases filed on paper before SUMAC stay on paper. Sign as “f/full name of the attorney”; an electronic signature equals a handwritten one. A document signed by a non-lawyer is filed with the handwritten signature.","c":"Reglas 2.1 y 69(A) Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Not filed until the fee is paid","d":"The date the filing fee (arancel) is paid is the filing date. The SUMAC receipt (comprobante) says the document is not officially filed until fees are paid, and the filing date does not relate back to the upload. Paying at the deadline is risky: pay electronically in SUMAC the same day. Note that the SUMAC directives treat fee-bearing filings as an exception to the rule that anything filed by 11:59 p.m. counts as filed that day; the Reglamento TA does not say a fee paid after business hours fails, but leave a margin.","c":"Reglas 14(A), 33(A), 58(A) y 72(D) Reglamento TA; Directrices SUMAC, Secs. VII y XVI"},{"t":"File formats","d":"Each main document and each attachment must be a legible PDF of no more than 4 MB. Scans should be black and white, both sides, portrait orientation. Electronic filings still must meet the form rules of the Reglamento TA.","c":"Directrices SUMAC, Sec. VIII"},{"t":"When SUMAC fails","d":"Problems with your own equipment never excuse any term. If the platform is down or in maintenance, file in person at the Secretaría or in the drop box before the term expires (8:30 a.m. to 5:00 p.m.). The Courts Administration Office may announce that filings can be emailed as PDFs, and the TA has its own SUMAC email address for that. If the problem is not the platform’s, a filing by email or in person is allowed only when authorized and must come with a motion describing your attempts (Form OAT 1728). Serve the filing on the other parties by email or post.","c":"Directrices SUMAC, Sec. XIX; Regla 2.1 Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Telling the lower court","d":"If the TPI case has an electronic record, SUMAC automatically posts an entry in it, which satisfies the duty to notify the lower court. If not, file the stamped cover or first page with the TPI Secretaría within 72 hours (civil appeal) or 48 hours (criminal appeal, certiorari). On paper: a civil appeal filed at the TA requires notice to the TPI within 72 hours; a recurso filed at the TPI must be sent in original to the TA within 48 hours. All of these are strict-compliance terms.","c":"Reglas 14, 24 y 33(A) Reglamento TA; Regla 194 R. Proc. Crim."},{"t":"The Tribunal Supremo still describes paper filing","d":"The Reglamento TS (through ER-2023-0001) requires an original and 10 copies, with an option to file appendix copies on CD in PDF (plus the brief in PDF and Word). It has not been updated for SUMAC, and Consi’s sources do not say whether the TS now accepts SUMAC filings. Check with the TS Secretaría before filing.","c":"Regla 40(d) Reglamento TS"},{"t":"Keep your RUA email current","d":"TA decisions are notified to attorneys at the email on file in the Registro Único de Abogados y Abogadas (RUA), and all electronic communications between counsel and with the court go to that address.","c":"Reglas 14(D), 15 y 71(B) Reglamento TA; Regla 9(j) Reglamento TS"}]],["Serving (notifying) the other parties",[{"t":"In the TA, e-filing serves the lawyers, but not everyone","d":"Filing a recurso in SUMAC is service on counsel (and, in criminal cases and criminal certiorari, on the Procurador General and the District Attorney). The platform then lists who was notified; check that list. You must yourself serve self-represented parties and anyone else the law requires. Serve the recurso with its appendix within the filing term (strict compliance).","c":"Reglas 13(B), 23(B) y 33(B) Reglamento TA"},{"t":"When electronic service is not possible","d":"Serve a copy stamped with the filing date and time by certified mail or private courier with return receipt; ordinary mail, personal delivery or email (to an address in the TPI record) also work if the copy is exact and there is no dispute about the date or receipt. The service date is the SUMAC receipt date, the mailing date, or the courier’s intake date. For certiorari served by personal delivery, certify the manner and circumstances within 48 hours.","c":"Reglas 13(B)(2)–(3) y 33(B) Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Certify how you served","d":"State in the recurso how and when you served; your signature certifies it. Report any change in a supplementary motion within 48 hours after filing (3 business days in administrative revisión). Both are strict-compliance terms. Even with e-filing, lawyers must still state the method of service.","c":"Reglas 13(B)(1), 15, 58(B)(4) y 69(B) Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Administrative revisión: serve the agency too","d":"Serve the stamped recurso on counsel of record in the agency proceeding (or the parties) and on the agency or official whose decision is challenged, within the filing term (strict compliance).","c":"Regla 58(B) Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Later filings","d":"Every later filing is served at the same time it is filed, through the platform or, if that is not possible, by personal delivery, mail, courier or email, and the filing states how. Motions are served on the day they are filed. In criminal cases, later motions need not go to the District Attorney unless a rule or the court requires it.","c":"Reglas 24(C), 36, 68(A) y 71(A) Reglamento TA"},{"t":"In the TS","d":"Serve every party’s counsel (or unrepresented parties) within the term to file, by certified mail or courier with return receipt or by email; mailed service goes to the attorney’s RUA address and is dated by the mailing. Certify personal delivery within 72 hours. Also give the TA Secretaría a stamped copy of the recurso without the appendix within 72 hours (strict compliance).","c":"Reglas 17(b), 20(d)–(e) y 39 Reglamento TS"}]],["The appendix (apéndice)",[{"t":"What goes in a TA appendix","d":"For an apelación or certiorari: the pleadings (complaint, cross-claims, third-party complaints, counterclaims and answers; in criminal certiorari, the complaint and accusation); the decision under review with its findings and the notice of its archivo en autos; every motion (showing the TPI filing date), resolution and order needed to prove that the term was interrupted and restarted, with their notices; every filing or ruling in the TPI record that discusses an issue raised; and anything else in the record useful to the court. If you claim evidence was wrongly excluded, put the offered evidence in a separate appendix.","c":"Reglas 16(E), 34(E) y 74(C) Reglamento TA"},{"t":"A missing document is not automatically fatal","d":"The TA may allow late filing of omitted appendix documents, on request in the recurso, by motion or on its own, within 15 days after notice of the order allowing it. If you do not cure within that time, the recurso may be dismissed.","c":"Reglas 16(E)(2) y 34(E)(2) Reglamento TA"},{"t":"How to build it in SUMAC","d":"Arrange documents chronologically. In an electronic case, the index points to the SUMAC entry of each document; SUMAC displays the entries you select, and that screen is the index, so you need not upload a separate one. Cite record documents by entry number and page (for example, “Ent. #123”). On paper, number pages consecutively and add an index of pages. Include only documents in the TPI or agency record, and do not repeat a document already in an earlier appendix in the same case. With leave, a voluminous appendix may be filed on one CD or USB in PDF only, or you may ask that the original record be sent up.","c":"Reglas 37(C), 70(G) y 74 Reglamento TA"},{"t":"What goes in a TS appendix","d":"An exact and complete copy of: the recurso filed in the TA, the opposition and all parties’ briefs, with their appendices; the judgment or resolution under review with the notice (volante) of its archivo en autos, plus the envelope if the mailing date differs; any reconsideration ruling with its notice; any document needed to show jurisdiction, with a legible filing stamp; the transcript or narrative statement if the errors concern the evidence; and any related ruling of a U.S. federal or state court (its omission can lead to denial). Number pages consecutively, in chronological order, with an index.","c":"Reglas 17(d)(5), 20(g)(4) y 34 Reglamento TS"}]],["Page limits and format",[{"t":"TA page limits","d":"Apelación, certiorari, revisión, habeas corpus and mandamus petitions, certiorari oppositions, show-cause responses, and the briefs of appellees, respondents and intervenors: 25 pages in a paper case, 35 pages in an electronic case, not counting the certificate of service, index and appendix. Criminal escrito de apelación (the notice): 3 pages on paper, 6 electronic, not counting cover and certificate. Criminal briefs: 25/35. Motions: 10 pages. Amicus motions: 5 pages. To exceed a limit, file a separate motion showing good cause with the brief; otherwise it will not be accepted.","c":"Reglas 16(D), 26(D), 28(D), 34(D), 37(A), 38(A), 55(E), 59(D), 63(A), 68(D), 70(D), 73(E) y 81(C) Reglamento TA"},{"t":"TA format","d":"Electronic filings: letter-size pages (8.5 × 11 in.). Paper cases: legal size (8.5 × 14 in.), printed on one side. Both: double-spaced, pica or larger type (except block quotes and footnotes), Courier or a similar 12-point font, left margin at least 1.5 in., right margin at least 0.5 in. No separate memorandum of authorities: argue the law in the body. The index is a table of contents that sets out each issue in full; the old index of authorities is no longer required.","c":"Reglas 16(C)(2), 70(A), 70(E) y 75 Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Required contents of a TA recurso","d":"Cover (with the Judicial Region; in electronic cases leave off the TA case number, which SUMAC assigns), index, then in order: who appears, the provisions giving jurisdiction, the decision under review with its date and the archivo en autos date and any motions that interrupted the term, the facts, the errors assigned, the discussion, and the relief requested. Since 2025 you must also disclose any related recurso in the TA or TS, and keep telling the TA by motion if you learn of one later. Attorneys include postal address, phone, email and TS (RUA) number.","c":"Reglas 16(A)–(C) y 34(A)–(C) Reglamento TA"},{"t":"TS page limits and format","d":"Certiorari petition or appeal: 25 pages, not counting index and appendix. Opposition to certiorari: 25 pages. Briefs (alegatos) after the writ issues or the appeal is accepted: 50 pages. Show-cause responses: 25 pages. Reconsideration: 10 pages. Times New Roman, Courier New or Arial 12-point, double-spaced, legal-size paper on one side, margins of at least 0.5 in.; original and 10 copies. The TS still requires an index of authorities.","c":"Reglas 17(d)(2), (4), 20(f)–(g), 20(l), 33(f), 40, 45(b) y 46(b) Reglamento TS"}]],["Language and translation",[{"t":"No general language rule in the appellate rules Consi has","d":"Neither the Reglamento TA nor the Reglamento TS, as provided to Consi, contains a general rule on the language of briefs; both courts’ rules and forms are in Spanish. In the TPI, pleadings, applications and motions may be in Spanish or English, documents in English need not be translated unless the court orders it (when justice requires, when translation is indispensable to a fair decision, or when a party asks), and a party who knows neither language may file in their own language with Spanish or English copies.","c":"Regla 8.7 R. Proc. Civ."},{"t":"TS: certified questions and U.S. Supreme Court review","d":"In certification from another jurisdiction, briefs may be in English or Spanish without translation, and the TS opinion is translated into English with the cost split among the parties unless otherwise ordered. An indigent litigant seeking U.S. Supreme Court review of a TS judgment may ask the TS for free certified English translations of the designated record.","c":"Reglas 25(h), 25(l) y 47 Reglamento TS"}]],["Oral evidence (transcripts) in the TA",[{"t":"Say within 10 days whether you will reproduce the testimony","d":"Within 10 days after filing the recurso, tell the TA whether you will reproduce the oral evidence and by what method (transcript, stipulated narrative statement or other), identifying the portions, dates and witnesses. If an error depends on the evidence and you say nothing, the TA must order you to state your intent and warn that errors depending on the testimony may be deemed waived. The TA may instead let the parties submit the re-recording (regrabación) itself.","c":"Reglas 19, 29 y 76(A) Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Transcript and narrative timetable","d":"Once authorized, ask the lower court for the re-recording within 10 days after notice of the TA order (with fees). Prepare the transcript at your own cost within 30 days after receiving the re-recording, certified by the transcriber, with a witness index, and serve copies within the same time. For a stipulated narrative: draft to the other parties within 30 days of authorization, then 20 days to agree; failing agreement, the other side files specific objections. Transcription at public expense is only for indigent appellants in criminal appeals. A supplementary brief may follow within 30 days after the transcript or narrative is filed (say so when you file it).","c":"Reglas 21 y 76(B)–(D) Reglamento TA"}]],["What changed on June 16, 2025 (Resolución ER-2025-0001)",[{"t":"The rewrite in one paragraph","d":"Resolución ER-2025-0001 (approved April 24, 2025; in force June 16, 2025; applies to pending cases) amended 56 rules, added Reglas 2.1 and 80.1, and folded Regla 76.1 into Regla 76 to fit the TA’s move to SUMAC and to speed up appellate practice. The points below come from the “Comentario – Enmiendas del 2025” notes in the official text. Resolución ER-2025-0003 (December 19, 2025) also amended the Reglamento; Consi has its result in the consolidated text but not the resolution itself, so it cannot say which rules it changed.","c":"Resolución ER-2025-0001; Regla 89 Reglamento TA (Comentario – Enmiendas del 2025)"},{"t":"E-filing, service and timing","d":"New Regla 2.1 makes SUMAC filing the rule and equal to filing in the Secretaría. The SUMAC send date is the archivo en autos. The fee-payment date is the filing date. E-filing a recurso serves opposing counsel, and SUMAC notifies the TPI automatically when the TPI case is electronic. Electronic filings are due at 11:59 p.m. (formerly 5:00 p.m.) on any day. The requirement to file 3 paper copies is gone. Electronic signatures (“f/name”) are recognized. Fax was removed as a service method.","c":"Reglas 2.1, 13, 14, 23, 24, 33, 69 y 72 Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Briefs and appendices","d":"Page limits rose from 25 to 35 for electronic cases because pages dropped from legal to letter size. The index of authorities was eliminated. Record documents are cited by SUMAC entry (“Ent. #”), and SUMAC generates the appendix index. A voluminous appendix may go on CD or USB with leave. Recursos must disclose related cases, and the duty continues after filing. The criminal notice of appeal may now run 6 pages when electronic.","c":"Reglas 16, 26, 34, 37, 70, 73, 74 y 75 Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Procedure","d":"Rules 76 and 76.1 on oral evidence were merged; the court must now ask about reproducing the evidence and warn of waiver, and may accept the re-recording instead of a transcript. New Regla 80.1 requires consolidation of recursos against the same ruling or arising from the same case. Regla 83(C) now says jurisdiction depends only on jurisdictional or strict-compliance requirements. Regla 79(C) lets the TA issue aid-of-jurisdiction orders despite non-jurisdictional defects. Reconsideration of an interlocutory ruling by a special (holiday) panel goes to the panel assigned to the case, and the TA may send the mandate early. Regla 32 dropped the old municipal-complaints certiorari and expressly lists protection orders; Regla 57 now cites the LPAU (Ley 38-2017); Part V was updated to the Código Electoral de 2020.","c":"Reglas 32, 41, 42, 57, 76, 79, 80.1, 83 y 84 Reglamento TA"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Civil appeal (apelación) to the TA","d":"30 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of judgment (SUMAC send date; mailing date if later).\nJurisdictional. Restarts after a timely Rule 43.1, 47 or 48 motion is decided and notified.","c":"Regla 13(A) Reglamento TA; Regla 52.2(a) R. Proc. Civ.","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of judgment)","note":"Jurisdictional. Restarts after a timely Rule 43.1, 47 or 48 motion is decided and notified."}},{"t":"Civil appeal to the TA when the government is a party","d":"60 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of judgment.\nJurisdictional. Applies to any aggrieved party when the ELA, its officials, a non-public-corporation instrumentality, or a municipality or its officials is a party.","c":"Regla 13(A) Reglamento TA; Regla 52.2(c) R. Proc. Civ.","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of judgment)","note":"Jurisdictional. Applies to any aggrieved party when the ELA, its officials, a non-public-corporation instrumentality, or a municipality or its officials is a party."}},{"t":"Certiorari to the TA against interlocutory orders (civil or criminal), protection orders, and judgments on arbitration-award review","d":"30 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the order (Civ. Rule 52.2(b) says “fecha de notificación”).\nStrict compliance: excusable only for special circumstances explained in the petition. No 60-day government extension.","c":"Regla 32(C) Reglamento TA; Regla 52.2(b) R. Proc. Civ.","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the order)","note":"Strict compliance: excusable only for special circumstances explained in the petition. No 60-day government extension."}},{"t":"Certiorari to the TA against final resolutions in jurisdicción voluntaria","d":"30 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the resolution.\nJurisdictional, unless a special law sets another term.","c":"Regla 32(B) Reglamento TA; Regla 52.2(b) R. Proc. Civ.","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the resolution)","note":"Jurisdictional, unless a special law sets another term."}},{"t":"Certiorari to the TA against a guilty-plea conviction","d":"30 days from date the sentence was dictated (mailing date of notice if the defendant was not present).\nJurisdictional. Criminal rules compilation current only to Feb. 2020.","c":"Regla 32(A) Reglamento TA; Regla 193 R. Proc. Crim.","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (date the sentence was dictated)","note":"Jurisdictional. Criminal rules compilation current only to Feb. 2020."}},{"t":"Criminal appeal to the TA","d":"30 days from date the sentence was dictated; or notice of the order denying a timely new-trial motion (Rules 188(e), 192) or deciding a timely reconsideration.\nJurisdictional. Regla 23 counts from notice to the accused of the ruling; Rule 194 counts a reconsideration ruling from its archivo en autos. Criminal rules compilation current only to Feb. 2020.","c":"Regla 23(A) Reglamento TA; Regla 194 R. Proc. Crim.","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (date the sentence was dictated; or notice of the order denying a timely new-trial motion)","note":"Jurisdictional. Regla 23 counts from notice to the accused of the ruling; Rule 194 counts a reconsideration ruling from its archivo en autos. Criminal rules compilation current only to Feb. 2020."}},{"t":"Recurso de revisión (final agency decision) to the TA","d":"30 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the final order (mailing date if later), or the applicable date after a timely reconsideration.\nJurisdictional unless a special law provides otherwise. Agency reconsideration terms are in the LPAU, not reviewed here.","c":"Regla 57 Reglamento TA","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the final order)","note":"Jurisdictional unless a special law provides otherwise. Agency reconsideration terms are in the LPAU, not reviewed here."}},{"t":"Recurso de revisión especial (welfare benefits, pro se)","d":"30 days from receipt of the agency’s final order.\nOnly for self-represented persons challenging welfare-benefit decisions.","c":"Regla 67(C) Reglamento TA","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (receipt of the agency’s final order)","note":"Only for self-represented persons challenging welfare-benefit decisions."}},{"t":"Electoral certiorari to the TA (CEE cases)","d":"10 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the TPI judgment (or of the ruling on reconsideration).\nJurisdictional. Within 30 days before an election: 48 hours. Within 5 days before: 24 hours, with same-day notice. No reconsideration in those windows.","c":"Regla 42 Reglamento TA","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the TPI judgment)","note":"Jurisdictional. Within 30 days before an election: 48 hours. Within 5 days before: 24 hours, with same-day notice. No reconsideration in those windows."}},{"t":"Serve the recurso and appendix on the other parties (TA)","d":"Within the filing term\nStrict compliance. E-filing serves counsel; you must serve self-represented and other required parties.","c":"Reglas 13(B)(1), 23(B), 33(B) y 58(B)(1) Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Notify the TPI when its case has no electronic record","d":"72 hours (civil appeal); 48 hours (criminal appeal, certiorari)\nStrict compliance. Not needed when SUMAC posts the entry automatically in an electronic TPI record.","c":"Reglas 14(A), 24(A) y 33(A) Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Send the original to the TA after filing on paper at the TPI","d":"48 hours\nStrict compliance. Only when paper filing is allowed under Regla 2.1.","c":"Reglas 14(C), 24(B) y 33(A) Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Supplementary certification of service","d":"48 hours (3 business days in revisión)\nStrict compliance.","c":"Reglas 15 y 58(B)(4) Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Late appendix documents, with leave","d":"15 days from notice of the TA resolution allowing late filing.\nFailure to cure may lead to dismissal.","c":"Reglas 16(E)(2) y 34(E)(2) Reglamento TA","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (notice of the TA resolution allowing late filing)","note":"Failure to cure may lead to dismissal."}},{"t":"Notice of intent to reproduce oral evidence","d":"10 days from filing of the recurso.\nIdentify portions, dates and witnesses, and the method proposed.","c":"Reglas 19(B), 29(B) y 76(A) Reglamento TA","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the recurso)","note":"Identify portions, dates and witnesses, and the method proposed."}},{"t":"Motion in the TPI or agency for the re-recording (regrabación)","d":"10 days from notice of the TA order authorizing reproduction.\nPay the fees with the motion.","c":"Regla 76(B) Reglamento TA","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (notice of the TA order authorizing reproduction)","note":"Pay the fees with the motion."}},{"t":"Transcript of oral evidence","d":"30 days from delivery of the re-recording.\nOr the term the TA orders; extendable only for good cause by a reasoned motion. Serve copies within the same term.","c":"Regla 76(C) Reglamento TA","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (delivery of the re-recording)","note":"Or the term the TA orders; extendable only for good cause by a reasoned motion. Serve copies within the same term."}},{"t":"Stipulated narrative statement: draft, then negotiation","d":"30, then 20\nIf no agreement, the other side files specific objections.","c":"Regla 76(D) Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Supplementary brief after transcript or narrative (civil appeal)","d":"30 days from filing of the transcript or narrative statement.\nAnnounce it when filing the transcript. The appellee may reply within 30 days.","c":"Regla 21 Reglamento TA","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the transcript or narrative statement)","note":"Announce it when filing the transcript. The appellee may reply within 30 days."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief, civil appeal (TA)","d":"30 days from filing of the escrito de apelación; or filing of the reproduced oral evidence if any is brought up.\nMay include its own appendix.","c":"Regla 22 Reglamento TA","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the escrito de apelación; or filing of the reproduced oral evidence if any is brought up)","note":"May include its own appendix."}},{"t":"Opposition to certiorari (TA)","d":"10 days from notification of the petition.\n25 pages paper / 35 electronic.","c":"Regla 37(A) Reglamento TA","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (notification of the petition)","note":"25 pages paper / 35 electronic."}},{"t":"Respondent’s brief after the TA issues certiorari","d":"30 days from notice of issuance of the writ.\nExtendable. The petition is the petitioner’s brief.","c":"Regla 39(A) Reglamento TA","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (notice of issuance of the writ)","note":"Extendable. The petition is the petitioner’s brief."}},{"t":"Opposition brief in administrative revisión","d":"30 days from notification of the recurso.\nDue from the agency, the official and other parties.","c":"Regla 63(A) Reglamento TA","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (notification of the recurso)","note":"Due from the agency, the official and other parties."}},{"t":"Criminal appellant’s brief; then El Pueblo’s brief","d":"30, then 30\nUnless the TA orders otherwise.","c":"Regla 28(A), (E) Reglamento TA"},{"t":"Response to a motion (TA)","d":"10 days from notification of the motion.\nThe TA may rule sooner in extraordinary circumstances.","c":"Regla 68(B) Reglamento TA","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (notification of the motion)","note":"The TA may rule sooner in extraordinary circumstances."}},{"t":"Request for an extension (TA or TS)","d":"At least 3 business days before the term expires\nNever available for jurisdictional terms.","c":"Regla 72(B) Reglamento TA; Regla 48(b) Reglamento TS"},{"t":"Panel review of a single judge’s habeas corpus or mandamus resolution","d":"10 days from archivo en autos of the notice of the resolution.","c":"Regla 55(H) Reglamento TA","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (archivo en autos of the notice of the resolution)","note":""}},{"t":"Reconsideration of a TPI civil judgment","d":"15 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of judgment.\nJurisdictional. Serve the other parties within the same 15 days (strict compliance). Must be specific or it does not interrupt.","c":"Regla 47 R. Proc. Civ.","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of judgment)","note":"Jurisdictional. Serve the other parties within the same 15 days (strict compliance). Must be specific or it does not interrupt."}},{"t":"Reconsideration of a TPI interlocutory order","d":"15 days from notice of the order.\nStrict compliance.","c":"Regla 47 R. Proc. Civ.","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (notice of the order)","note":"Strict compliance."}},{"t":"Reconsideration of a criminal sentence or conviction (TPI)","d":"15 days from date the sentence was dictated.\nNot extendable. Criminal rules compilation current only to Feb. 2020.","c":"Regla 194 R. Proc. Crim.","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (date the sentence was dictated)","note":"Not extendable. Criminal rules compilation current only to Feb. 2020."}},{"t":"Reconsideration in the TA","d":"15 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the TA decision.\nNot extendable. Suspends the term to go to the TS. Opposition within 10 days. Electoral cases: 10 days under Regla 42.","c":"Regla 84(A) Reglamento TA; Regla 216 R. Proc. Crim.","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the TA decision)","note":"Not extendable. Suspends the term to go to the TS. Opposition within 10 days. Electoral cases: 10 days under Regla 42."}},{"t":"Certiorari to the TS against a TA judgment in an appeal","d":"30 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice (or of the ruling on reconsideration; mailing date if later).\nJurisdictional. 60 days in civil cases where the government is a party.","c":"Regla 20(a)(1), (c) Reglamento TS; Regla 52.2(b) R. Proc. Civ.","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (archivo en autos of the copy of the notice)","note":"Jurisdictional. 60 days in civil cases where the government is a party."}},{"t":"Certiorari to the TS against TA rulings on guilty-plea certiorari, jurisdicción voluntaria, or administrative revisión","d":"30 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice (or of the ruling on reconsideration).\nJurisdictional.","c":"Regla 20(a)(2), (3), (5) Reglamento TS","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (archivo en autos of the copy of the notice)","note":"Jurisdictional."}},{"t":"Certiorari to the TS against other TA rulings in discretionary recursos, or TA interlocutory resolutions","d":"30 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice (Civ. Rule 52.2(b) says “fecha de notificación”).\nStrict compliance. In criminal cases, Rule 217 R. Proc. Crim. calls the 30-day term for certiorari against a TA judgment or final resolution denying certiorari jurisdictional; treat it as jurisdictional.","c":"Regla 20(b)(1) Reglamento TS; Regla 52.2(b) R. Proc. Civ.","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (archivo en autos of the copy of the notice)","note":"Strict compliance. In criminal cases, Rule 217 R. Proc. Crim. calls the 30-day term for certiorari against a TA judgment or final resolution denying certiorari jurisdictional; treat it as jurisdictional."}},{"t":"Direct appeal to the TS (unconstitutionality or conflicting TA decisions)","d":"30 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the TA judgment (or of the ruling on reconsideration).\nJurisdictional. 60 days in civil cases where the government is a party.","c":"Regla 17(a) Reglamento TS","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the TA judgment)","note":"Jurisdictional. 60 days in civil cases where the government is a party."}},{"t":"Notify the TA of a recurso filed in the TS","d":"72 hours\nStrict compliance. Stamped copy without the appendix.","c":"Reglas 17(b) y 20(d) Reglamento TS"},{"t":"Opposition to certiorari (TS)","d":"10 days from notification of the petition.\n25 pages.","c":"Regla 20(l) Reglamento TS","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (notification of the petition)","note":"25 pages."}},{"t":"Briefs in the TS after the writ issues or the appeal is accepted","d":"30, then 30\nRegla 33(i) counts the second 30 days from filing of the other side’s brief; Reglas 19 and 21 from receipt. Use the earlier date.","c":"Reglas 19, 21 y 33(i) Reglamento TS"},{"t":"Reconsideration in the TS","d":"10 business days\nJurisdictional; 10 pages; no extensions. A second motion only within 3 business days after notice of the denial.","c":"Regla 45(a)–(b) Reglamento TS"},{"t":"Electoral certiorari to the TS","d":"10 days from archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the TA judgment (or of the ruling on reconsideration).\nJurisdictional. Reconsideration within 3 days. The TS rule still cites the 2011 Electoral Code; the Reglamento TA (Regla 41) refers to it as “Regla 21” of the Reglamento TS.","c":"Regla 22(a) Reglamento TS","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the TA judgment)","note":"Jurisdictional. Reconsideration within 3 days. The TS rule still cites the 2011 Electoral Code; the Reglamento TA (Regla 41) refers to it as “Regla 21” of the Reglamento TS."}},{"t":"TA mandate to the TPI or agency","d":"10 business days\nSince 2025 the TA may send it earlier for cause.","c":"Regla 84(E) Reglamento TA"}]]],"dl":[["Civil appeal (apelación) to the TA",30,"Archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of judgment (SUMAC send date; mailing date if later)","Regla 13(A) Reglamento TA; Regla 52.2(a) R. Proc. Civ.","Jurisdictional. Restarts after a timely Rule 43.1, 47 or 48 motion is decided and notified."],["Civil appeal to the TA when the government is a party",60,"Archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of judgment","Regla 13(A) Reglamento TA; Regla 52.2(c) R. Proc. Civ.","Jurisdictional. Applies to any aggrieved party when the ELA, its officials, a non-public-corporation instrumentality, or a municipality or its officials is a party."],["Certiorari to the TA against interlocutory orders (civil or criminal), protection orders, and judgments on arbitration-award review",30,"Archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the order (Civ. Rule 52.2(b) says “fecha de notificación”)","Regla 32(C) Reglamento TA; Regla 52.2(b) R. Proc. Civ.","Strict compliance: excusable only for special circumstances explained in the petition. No 60-day government extension."],["Certiorari to the TA against final resolutions in jurisdicción voluntaria",30,"Archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the resolution","Regla 32(B) Reglamento TA; Regla 52.2(b) R. Proc. Civ.","Jurisdictional, unless a special law sets another term."],["Certiorari to the TA against a guilty-plea conviction",30,"Date the sentence was dictated (mailing date of notice if the defendant was not present)","Regla 32(A) Reglamento TA; Regla 193 R. Proc. Crim.","Jurisdictional. Criminal rules compilation current only to Feb. 2020."],["Criminal appeal to the TA",30,"Date the sentence was dictated; or notice of the order denying a timely new-trial motion (Rules 188(e), 192) or deciding a timely reconsideration","Regla 23(A) Reglamento TA; Regla 194 R. Proc. Crim.","Jurisdictional. Regla 23 counts from notice to the accused of the ruling; Rule 194 counts a reconsideration ruling from its archivo en autos. Criminal rules compilation current only to Feb. 2020."],["Recurso de revisión (final agency decision) to the TA",30,"Archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the final order (mailing date if later), or the applicable date after a timely reconsideration","Regla 57 Reglamento TA","Jurisdictional unless a special law provides otherwise. Agency reconsideration terms are in the LPAU, not reviewed here."],["Recurso de revisión especial (welfare benefits, pro se)",30,"Receipt of the agency’s final order","Regla 67(C) Reglamento TA","Only for self-represented persons challenging welfare-benefit decisions."],["Electoral certiorari to the TA (CEE cases)",10,"Archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the TPI judgment (or of the ruling on reconsideration)","Regla 42 Reglamento TA","Jurisdictional. Within 30 days before an election: 48 hours. Within 5 days before: 24 hours, with same-day notice. No reconsideration in those windows."],["Serve the recurso and appendix on the other parties (TA)","Within the filing term","Same trigger as the recurso","Reglas 13(B)(1), 23(B), 33(B) y 58(B)(1) Reglamento TA","Strict compliance. E-filing serves counsel; you must serve self-represented and other required parties."],["Notify the TPI when its case has no electronic record","72 hours (civil appeal); 48 hours (criminal appeal, certiorari)","Filing of the recurso","Reglas 14(A), 24(A) y 33(A) Reglamento TA","Strict compliance. Not needed when SUMAC posts the entry automatically in an electronic TPI record."],["Send the original to the TA after filing on paper at the TPI","48 hours","Filing at the TPI Secretaría","Reglas 14(C), 24(B) y 33(A) Reglamento TA","Strict compliance. Only when paper filing is allowed under Regla 2.1."],["Supplementary certification of service","48 hours (3 business days in revisión)","Filing of the recurso","Reglas 15 y 58(B)(4) Reglamento TA","Strict compliance."],["Late appendix documents, with leave",15,"Notice of the TA resolution allowing late filing","Reglas 16(E)(2) y 34(E)(2) Reglamento TA","Failure to cure may lead to dismissal."],["Notice of intent to reproduce oral evidence",10,"Filing of the recurso","Reglas 19(B), 29(B) y 76(A) Reglamento TA","Identify portions, dates and witnesses, and the method proposed."],["Motion in the TPI or agency for the re-recording (regrabación)",10,"Notice of the TA order authorizing reproduction","Regla 76(B) Reglamento TA","Pay the fees with the motion."],["Transcript of oral evidence",30,"Delivery of the re-recording","Regla 76(C) Reglamento TA","Or the term the TA orders; extendable only for good cause by a reasoned motion. Serve copies within the same term."],["Stipulated narrative statement: draft, then negotiation","30, then 20","TA authorization; then service of the draft","Regla 76(D) Reglamento TA","If no agreement, the other side files specific objections."],["Supplementary brief after transcript or narrative (civil appeal)",30,"Filing of the transcript or narrative statement","Regla 21 Reglamento TA","Announce it when filing the transcript. The appellee may reply within 30 days."],["Appellee’s brief, civil appeal (TA)",30,"Filing of the escrito de apelación; or filing of the reproduced oral evidence if any is brought up","Regla 22 Reglamento TA","May include its own appendix."],["Opposition to certiorari (TA)",10,"Notification of the petition","Regla 37(A) Reglamento TA","25 pages paper / 35 electronic."],["Respondent’s brief after the TA issues certiorari",30,"Notice of issuance of the writ","Regla 39(A) Reglamento TA","Extendable. The petition is the petitioner’s brief."],["Opposition brief in administrative revisión",30,"Notification of the recurso","Regla 63(A) Reglamento TA","Due from the agency, the official and other parties."],["Criminal appellant’s brief; then El Pueblo’s brief","30, then 30","Elevation of the record; then filing of the appellant’s brief","Regla 28(A), (E) Reglamento TA","Unless the TA orders otherwise."],["Response to a motion (TA)",10,"Notification of the motion","Regla 68(B) Reglamento TA","The TA may rule sooner in extraordinary circumstances."],["Request for an extension (TA or TS)","At least 3 business days before the term expires","Expiration of the term","Regla 72(B) Reglamento TA; Regla 48(b) Reglamento TS","Never available for jurisdictional terms."],["Panel review of a single judge’s habeas corpus or mandamus resolution",10,"Archivo en autos of the notice of the resolution","Regla 55(H) Reglamento TA",""],["Reconsideration of a TPI civil judgment",15,"Archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of judgment","Regla 47 R. Proc. Civ.","Jurisdictional. Serve the other parties within the same 15 days (strict compliance). Must be specific or it does not interrupt."],["Reconsideration of a TPI interlocutory order",15,"Notice of the order","Regla 47 R. Proc. Civ.","Strict compliance."],["Reconsideration of a criminal sentence or conviction (TPI)",15,"Date the sentence was dictated","Regla 194 R. Proc. Crim.","Not extendable. Criminal rules compilation current only to Feb. 2020."],["Reconsideration in the TA",15,"Archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the TA decision","Regla 84(A) Reglamento TA; Regla 216 R. Proc. Crim.","Not extendable. Suspends the term to go to the TS. Opposition within 10 days. Electoral cases: 10 days under Regla 42."],["Certiorari to the TS against a TA judgment in an appeal",30,"Archivo en autos of the copy of the notice (or of the ruling on reconsideration; mailing date if later)","Regla 20(a)(1), (c) Reglamento TS; Regla 52.2(b) R. Proc. Civ.","Jurisdictional. 60 days in civil cases where the government is a party."],["Certiorari to the TS against TA rulings on guilty-plea certiorari, jurisdicción voluntaria, or administrative revisión",30,"Archivo en autos of the copy of the notice (or of the ruling on reconsideration)","Regla 20(a)(2), (3), (5) Reglamento TS","Jurisdictional."],["Certiorari to the TS against other TA rulings in discretionary recursos, or TA interlocutory resolutions",30,"Archivo en autos of the copy of the notice (Civ. Rule 52.2(b) says “fecha de notificación”)","Regla 20(b)(1) Reglamento TS; Regla 52.2(b) R. Proc. Civ.","Strict compliance. In criminal cases, Rule 217 R. Proc. Crim. calls the 30-day term for certiorari against a TA judgment or final resolution denying certiorari jurisdictional; treat it as jurisdictional."],["Direct appeal to the TS (unconstitutionality or conflicting TA decisions)",30,"Archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the TA judgment (or of the ruling on reconsideration)","Regla 17(a) Reglamento TS","Jurisdictional. 60 days in civil cases where the government is a party."],["Notify the TA of a recurso filed in the TS","72 hours","Filing in the TS","Reglas 17(b) y 20(d) Reglamento TS","Strict compliance. Stamped copy without the appendix."],["Opposition to certiorari (TS)",10,"Notification of the petition","Regla 20(l) Reglamento TS","25 pages."],["Briefs in the TS after the writ issues or the appeal is accepted","30, then 30","Receipt of the Secretaría’s notice that the record was received (Regla 35(c)); then receipt of the petitioner’s or appellant’s brief","Reglas 19, 21 y 33(i) Reglamento TS","Regla 33(i) counts the second 30 days from filing of the other side’s brief; Reglas 19 and 21 from receipt. Use the earlier date."],["Reconsideration in the TS","10 business days","Date the decision was sent to the parties","Regla 45(a)–(b) Reglamento TS","Jurisdictional; 10 pages; no extensions. A second motion only within 3 business days after notice of the denial."],["Electoral certiorari to the TS",10,"Archivo en autos of the copy of the notice of the TA judgment (or of the ruling on reconsideration)","Regla 22(a) Reglamento TS","Jurisdictional. Reconsideration within 3 days. The TS rule still cites the 2011 Electoral Code; the Reglamento TA (Regla 41) refers to it as “Regla 21” of the Reglamento TS."],["TA mandate to the TPI or agency","10 business days","The TA decision becoming final and firm","Regla 84(E) Reglamento TA","Since 2025 the TA may send it earlier for cause."]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://poderjudicial.pr/"]]},"rulesName":"Puerto Rico Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our unofficial English summary. The official text is in Spanish; tap Read the official text.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope of the rules","s":"Govern all civil proceedings before the General Court of Justice (Tribunal General de Justicia), and are to be construed to ease access to the courts and case management so as to secure a just, speedy, and economical resolution of every proceeding.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"2","t":"Commencing an action","s":"A civil action begins by filing a complaint (demanda) in court.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"3","t":"Jurisdiction, venue, and transfer","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 3.1–3.6, which cover the court’s jurisdiction, the proper courtroom (sala) for filing, and transfer between salas.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"3.1","t":"Jurisdiction","s":"The General Court of Justice has jurisdiction over every matter arising within Puerto Rico and over domiciliaries and non-domiciliaries with contacts that make jurisdiction constitutionally permissible. It also hears voluntary-jurisdiction (ex parte) proceedings to record and perpetuate a fact that is not then in judicial controversy and cannot prejudice a specific person.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"3.2","t":"Venue (competencia)","s":"File in the sala designated by law and these rules, but no case is dismissed for being filed in the wrong sala. A case stays in the filing sala only by agreement of the parties plus the presiding judge’s reasoned consent; otherwise the judge transfers it to the proper sala.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"3.3","t":"Actions affecting real property","s":"Actions over title to or any right or interest in real property must be filed in the sala where the property, or part of it, is located.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"3.4","t":"Venue where the claim arose","s":"Actions against the Commonwealth, against insurance or surety companies, and for damages are filed in the sala where the insured or bonded object is located or where the cause of action, or part of it, arose.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"3.5","t":"Venue by residence of the parties","s":"Otherwise, file where a defendant resides; wage claims go to the plaintiff’s residence and child-support (alimentos) cases to the minors’ residence. If no defendant resides in Puerto Rico or the residence is unknown, any sala of the Court of First Instance may be used; businesses and entities with offices or agents in several places may be sued where they have their principal office, operations center, or agent, or where they incurred the obligation.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"3.6","t":"Transfer of actions","s":"A defendant challenging venue must move to transfer within 30 days after notice of the complaint and summons, stating the supporting facts in detail unless they appear on the face of the complaint or record; if no opposition is filed within 10 days after notice of the motion, the case is transferred. Filing other motions or a responsive pleading within those 30 days does not waive the right to seek transfer, and the court may also transfer for the convenience of witnesses or the interests of justice.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"4","t":"Summons (emplazamiento)","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 4.1–4.8 on issuing, serving, waiving, and proving service of the summons; Puerto Rico’s service regime (120 days under Rule 4.3(c), waiver under Rule 4.5, edicts under Rule 4.6) differs in important ways from federal Rule 4.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"4.1","t":"Issuance","s":"The plaintiff files the summons form with the complaint for immediate issuance by the Clerk (Secretaría), who will issue individual or additional summonses against any defendants on request.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"4.2","t":"Form of summons","s":"Signed by the Clerk, bearing the court’s name and seal, the sala, and the parties’ names; addressed to the defendant; and stating the name, postal address, telephone, fax, email, and Supreme Court attorney number of plaintiff’s counsel (or the plaintiff if pro se). It must state the time to appear and warn that a default judgment may be entered granting the relief requested or any other relief the court deems proper.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"4.3","t":"Who may serve; 120-day service deadline","s":"Service may be made by a marshal (alguacil) or any literate person at least 18 years old who is not a party, a party’s attorney, a relative within the fourth degree of consanguinity or second of affinity, or interested in the case; defendants outside Puerto Rico may be served personally, as the law of the place of service allows, by letters rogatory, by edict under Rule 4.6, or as the court directs. Under Rule 4.3(c) the summons must be served within 120 days after the complaint is filed or the edict summons is issued; the Clerk must issue summonses the same day the complaint is filed, and any delay in issuance is added to the time if the plaintiff timely seeks an extension. If service is not made in time, the court must dismiss without prejudice, and a second dismissal for missing this deadline operates as an adjudication on the merits The only extension the text expressly describes is that issuance-delay credit, which depends on a timely extension request.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"4.4","t":"Personal service","s":"The summons and complaint are served together, and the server notes on the back of the summons copy their signature, date, place, manner, and recipient. The rule sets the method for adults (personally or to an authorized or statutory agent), minors under 14 (parent with patria potestad or guardian), minors 14 and over (the minor and a parent or guardian), adjudged incapacitated persons (the person and guardian), people in mental-health institutions (the person and the director), prisoners (the person and the warden), entities (officer, managing agent, general agent, or authorized or statutory agent), and the conjugal partnership (both spouses). The Commonwealth is served through the Secretary of Justice or designee; for a non-public-corporation officer or agency, the officer or agency head must be served and serving the Secretary of Justice is also an indispensable requirement; municipalities are served through their chief executive or designee.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"4.5","t":"Waiver of personal service","s":"Adults and entities notified of a suit have a duty to avoid service costs, and waiving service does not waive jurisdictional defenses or a venue transfer. The plaintiff’s written request must go by certified mail with return receipt and restricted delivery, include a filed-and-stamped copy of the complaint, explain the consequences, and allow 20 days from mailing to return the signed waiver (30 days if the defendant is outside Puerto Rico), with an extra copy and a pre-addressed envelope. A defendant who returns the waiver must answer within 30 days after returning it; one who refuses without good cause pays service costs plus attorney’s fees for the cost motion; the waiver procedure cannot be used for the Commonwealth, its agencies, municipalities, or officials, or for minors or incapacitated persons.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"4.6","t":"Service by edict (publication)","s":"On a sworn statement of the diligent efforts made, the court may order service by edict when the defendant is outside Puerto Rico, cannot be found in Puerto Rico, is hiding, or is a foreign corporation without a resident agent, and the affidavit or complaint shows a claim justifying relief; a failed-service return is not required. The edict is published once in a newspaper of general circulation in Puerto Rico, and within 10 days after publication a copy of the summons and complaint must be sent by certified mail or other return-receipt delivery to the last known address, unless an affidavit shows no address could be found. The edict must contain listed items (title, sala, case number, parties, nature of suit, counsel’s contact information, issuer, issuance date, and the time to answer under Rule 10.1 with a default warning), with each first mention of a person in 10-point bold type; an amended complaint filed before appearance must be served the same way, and unknown defendants are served by edict.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"4.7","t":"Proof of service","s":"The server files proof within the time the defendant has to appear: a marshal’s certification or a private server’s sworn statement. Edict service is proved by the newspaper’s sworn statement, a copy of the published edict, and counsel’s certification of mailing (plus the defendant’s return receipt); service under Rule 4.3(b)(2) and (5) is proved by sworn statement or the judge’s order. Failure to file proof does not affect validity, and admission of service, waiver, or appearance makes proof unnecessary.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"4.8","t":"Amendment of summons","s":"The court may at any time allow amendment of a summons or the proof of service on just terms, unless doing so would clearly and substantially prejudice the served party’s essential rights.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"5","t":"Pleadings allowed","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 5.1–5.2.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"5.1","t":"Pleadings","s":"The only pleadings are the complaint, counterclaim, crossclaim (demanda contra coparte), third-party complaint, and their answers; the court may order a reply to an answer or third-party answer.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"5.2","t":"Action on stipulated facts","s":"Parties to an actual controversy may skip pleadings and submit a stipulation of facts with a sworn statement that a real controversy exists and the stipulation is made in good faith; if the court agrees a controversy exists, the rules then govern.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"6","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 6.1–6.6 on claims, denials, affirmative defenses, and extensions.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"6.1","t":"Claims for relief","s":"A pleading seeking relief must contain a short and plain statement of the facts showing the pleader is entitled to relief and a demand for the relief sought; alternative relief or relief of different kinds may be requested.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"6.2","t":"Defenses; form of denials","s":"The responding party must admit or deny each allegation and state its defenses with the supporting facts, and the court may order compliance. A party lacking knowledge must say so, investigate, and amend by the deadline set at the initial conference or by the pretrial conference date; allegations not later admitted or denied are deemed admitted. Denials must fairly meet the substance and affirmatively state the denying party’s version; a party may deny specifically or generally (except what it expressly admits), subject to Rule 9.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"6.3","t":"Affirmative defenses","s":"Listed defenses (settlement, accord and satisfaction, arbitration award, assumption of risk, negligence, discharge in bankruptcy, duress, estoppel, failure of consideration, fraud, illegality, laches, authorization, payment, release, res judicata, acquisitive or extinctive prescription, waiver, and any other excuse or affirmative defense) must be pleaded clearly, expressly, and specifically or are waived, unless the party learns of the defense during discovery, in which case it must amend. A mislabeled defense or counterclaim may be treated as properly designated.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"6.4","t":"Effect of failure to deny","s":"Allegations, other than the amount of damages, in a pleading that requires a response are deemed admitted if not denied; allegations in a pleading that requires no response are deemed denied.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"6.5","t":"Concise and direct pleading; inconsistency","s":"Each allegation must be simple, concise, and direct, with no technical forms required, and pleadings are construed to do justice; subject to Rule 9, a party may plead alternative or inconsistent claims or defenses.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"6.6","t":"Extensions of time","s":"Every extension request must show good cause (justa causa) with concrete, well-supported explanations and must be filed before the deadline expires and in conformity with Rule 68.2. The extension runs from the day after the original deadline expires.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 7.1–7.5.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"7.1","t":"Capacity","s":"Capacity, representative authority, and legal existence need not be alleged, but a party contesting them must do so specifically, with the particulars within its knowledge.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"7.2","t":"Fraud, mistake, or state of mind","s":"The circumstances of fraud or mistake must be pleaded in detail; malice, intent, knowledge, and other states of mind may be alleged generally.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"7.3","t":"Time and place","s":"Allegations of time and place are material for testing the sufficiency of a pleading.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"7.4","t":"Special damages","s":"Special damages must be itemized by category.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"7.5","t":"Description of real property","s":"A pleading claiming a right in real property must describe it precisely enough to identify it.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"8","t":"Form of pleadings and motions","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 8.1–8.9 on captions, motions, filings to the record, language, and corporate disclosure.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"8.1","t":"Caption","s":"Every pleading has a caption with the court and sala, the parties’ names, the filing number, and the nature or subject of the action; the complaint names all parties, later filings may name the first party on each side with a reference to others, and voluntary-jurisdiction filings put the petitioner’s full name above “Ex parte.”","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"8.2","t":"Paragraphs; separate statements","s":"Allegations go in numbered paragraphs, each limited as far as practicable to one set of circumstances; claims founded on separate occurrences and defenses other than denials are stated separately when that promotes clarity.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"8.3","t":"Adoption by reference; exhibits","s":"Statements may be adopted by reference within the same or another pleading or motion, and an exhibit attached to a pleading is part of it for all purposes.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"8.4","t":"Motions; 20-day opposition","s":"A request for an order is made by motion, in writing unless made at a hearing, stating with particularity the legal grounds and arguments and the relief sought, with any needed documents or affidavits. Any opposition, with its supporting documents, is due within 20 days after notice of the motion; if none is filed in that time, the motion is deemed submitted. Motions are decided without a hearing unless the court sets one or a law or rule requires one.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"8.5","t":"Motions to continue or reschedule a hearing","s":"Pretrial motions to continue or reschedule a hearing must be written and state grounds; an oral request on the hearing day is allowed only for extraordinary, unforeseeable circumstances beyond the control of the parties and counsel. The movant must propose at least three new dates after confirming the other side has no conflict, and any stipulation to continue needs the presiding judge’s approval.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"8.6","t":"Filings “to the judicial record”","s":"Parties may place on record matters that need no judicial attention at that stage by a filing titled “AL EXPEDIENTE JUDICIAL”; it is treated like a motion but is not referred to the judge, though the court may later take notice of its contents and filing date.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"8.7","t":"Language","s":"Pleadings and motions must be in Spanish or English; filings that must be signed by someone who knows neither may be in that person’s language with Spanish or English copies. Translation of English documents is not required, but the court must order translation when justice requires, when indispensable to fair adjudication, or when a party requests it.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"8.8","t":"Form of other papers","s":"Rules on captions, signatures, and form for pleadings apply to motions and other papers.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"8.9","t":"Corporate disclosure statement","s":"Every nongovernmental corporation must file, at its first appearance, a certification identifying every parent, subsidiary, and affiliate corporation owning 10% or more of its stock, or stating there is none, and must promptly disclose any later change in writing.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"9","t":"Legal representation","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 9.1–9.4 on signatures, appearance and withdrawal of counsel, attorney conduct, and self-representation.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"9.1","t":"Signing and contents of filings; sanctions","s":"Every filing by a represented party must be signed by at least one attorney of record with name, Puerto Rico Supreme Court attorney number, telephone, fax, postal address, and email as registered with the Supreme Court; counsel’s first filing must also give the client’s physical and postal address and telephone, and pro se parties sign and give their own contact information. Changes in contact information must be reported immediately by motion, and filings need not be sworn unless a law requires it. The signature certifies availability to comply with the court’s settings and orders, that the signer read the filing, and that after reasonable inquiry it is well grounded in fact and law and not filed to cause injustice, delay, oppression, or added cost; violations, and filings with false, defamatory, or indecorous content or offensive language, draw sanctions under Rule 9.3, including reasonable expenses and attorney’s fees.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"9.2","t":"Appearance and withdrawal of counsel","s":"An attorney taking on a pending case must file a notice motion with their Supreme Court number and contact information. To withdraw, counsel must file a written motion giving reasons, the client’s telephone and postal address, and a statement that the client was notified and the Code of Professional Ethics canons were met; the court may deny withdrawal in exceptional cases where a party’s rights would be seriously harmed or the case unduly delayed.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"9.3","t":"Attorney conduct","s":"Appearing at a hearing, conference, or proceeding unprepared may be treated as conduct obstructing the administration of justice. Under its inherent power, the court may on its own or on motion impose monetary or other sanctions or disqualify an attorney for such conduct or for breaching duties to the court, clients, or colleagues.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"9.4","t":"Self-representation","s":"Natural persons may represent themselves in ordinary civil cases if they are unrepresented, choose to do so voluntarily and knowingly (understanding they will be treated like represented parties), can adequately handle the case given its complexity, have the minimum knowledge to follow the rules and argue the law, and will not cause undue delay or disruption. The court must monitor these requirements throughout and may suspend self-representation and order the party to appear through counsel within a set period; a party switching to self-representation mid-case must also ask timely and expressly. Pro se parties face the same Rule 9.3 sanctions and procedural consequences as represented parties, and the court is not required to instruct them or appoint counsel, though it should do so when sound administration of justice warrants.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"10","t":"Defenses and objections","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 10.1–10.8 on answer deadlines, pre-answer motions, judgment on the pleadings, more definite statement, striking, and waiver of defenses.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"10.1","t":"Time to answer","s":"A defendant inside or outside Puerto Rico must serve the answer within 30 days after delivery of the summons and complaint, or after publication of the edict if served under Rule 4.6 (federal Rule 12 allows 21 days). Answers to crossclaims and replies to counterclaims are due within 10 days after notice. When the Commonwealth or municipalities, their officers, or instrumentalities (excluding public corporations) are parties, any party’s answer, crossclaim answer, or counterclaim reply is due within a non-extendable (improrrogable) 60 days after delivery of the summons and complaint. Serving a motion under these rules or Rule 36 changes the deadlines: if denied or deferred to trial, the pleading is due 10 days after notice of the order; if a more definite statement is ordered, the response is due 10 days after service of that statement.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"10.2","t":"How defenses are presented; motion to dismiss","s":"All defenses go in the responsive pleading, except that the pleader may raise by well-founded motion (1) lack of subject-matter jurisdiction, (2) lack of personal jurisdiction, (3) insufficient summons, (4) insufficient service of summons, (5) failure to state a claim that justifies relief, and (6) failure to join an indispensable party. Joining defenses does not waive any of them. If matters outside the pleading are presented on a defense (5) motion and not excluded, it is treated as a summary judgment motion under Rule 36 and all parties get a reasonable chance to present material.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"10.3","t":"Judgment on the pleadings","s":"After the pleadings close, any party may move for partial or total judgment on the pleadings, subject to Rule 42.3; if matters outside the pleadings are presented and not excluded, the motion becomes a Rule 36 summary judgment motion.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"10.4","t":"More definite statement","s":"A party may seek a more definite statement before responding to a pleading so vague or ambiguous that a response cannot reasonably be framed, pointing out the defects and the details wanted. If granted and not obeyed within 10 days after notice (or another period the court sets), the court may strike the pleading or enter another just order.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"10.5","t":"Motion to strike","s":"The court may strike insufficient defenses or redundant, immaterial, impertinent, or scandalous matter, and supporting documents, on its own at any time or on motion filed before responding, or within 10 days after service of the pleading if no response is allowed.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"10.6","t":"Preliminary determinations","s":"Rule 10.2 defenses (1)–(6), judgment-on-the-pleadings motions, and motions to strike are to be decided on the merits at or before the initial conference.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"10.7","t":"Consolidation of defenses","s":"A Rule 10 motion may be joined with other Rule 10 motions then available; a party who files a Rule 10 motion but omits an available defense that could be raised by motion cannot later move on the omitted defense, except lack of subject-matter jurisdiction (Rule 10.2(1)) and the Rule 10.8(b) defenses.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"10.8","t":"Waiver of defenses","s":"Lack of personal jurisdiction, insufficient summons, and insufficient service are waived if omitted from a consolidated Rule 10.7 motion, or if neither raised by Rule 10 motion nor included in a responsive pleading or an amendment allowed without leave under Rule 13.1. Failure to state a claim, failure to join an indispensable party under Rule 16, and failure to state a legal defense may be raised in any permitted pleading, by motion for judgment on the pleadings, or at trial. Whenever it appears the court lacks subject-matter jurisdiction, it must dismiss the action.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"11","t":"Counterclaims and crossclaims","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 11.1–11.7.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"11.1","t":"Compulsory counterclaims","s":"A pleading must state as a counterclaim any claim against an opposing party that arises from the act, omission, or event underlying the opposing party’s claim and does not require third parties over whom the court cannot obtain jurisdiction; it need not be asserted if it was already the subject of another pending action when the suit began.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"11.2","t":"Permissive counterclaims","s":"A pleading may assert as a counterclaim any claim against an opposing party not arising from the same act, omission, or event.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"11.3","t":"Scope of counterclaim","s":"A counterclaim may reduce or defeat the opposing claim and may seek greater or different relief.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"11.4","t":"Counterclaim maturing after pleading","s":"A counterclaim that matures after the party served its pleading may be asserted with the court’s permission.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"11.5","t":"Omitted counterclaim","s":"A counterclaim omitted through oversight, inadvertence, or excusable neglect, or when justice requires, may be added by amendment with leave of court.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"11.6","t":"Crossclaims (demanda contra coparte)","s":"A crossclaim may assert any claim arising from the act, omission, or event of the original complaint or a counterclaim, or relating to property that is the subject of the original complaint, including a claim that the coparty is or may be liable to the crossclaimant. It may be filed without leave within 30 days after the filing of all defendants’ answers; afterward, leave is required on a showing of good cause.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"11.7","t":"Additional parties","s":"Persons other than existing parties may be added to a counterclaim or crossclaim under Rules 16 and 17.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"12","t":"Third-party practice","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 12.1–12.2.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"12.1","t":"Third-party complaint by defendant","s":"A defendant may serve a summons and third-party complaint on a nonparty who is or may be liable to the defendant for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim, or who is or may be liable to any party. It may be filed without leave within 30 days after the filing of the answer to the complaint or the reply to a counterclaim; after that, leave is required on a showing of good cause. The third-party defendant answers under Rule 10, asserts counterclaims and crossclaims under Rule 11, may raise the third-party plaintiff’s defenses against the plaintiff and assert related claims against the plaintiff; any party may seek severance, a separate trial, or dismissal, judgment may be entered under Rule 42.3, and a third-party defendant may in turn implead others.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"12.2","t":"Third-party claim by plaintiff","s":"A plaintiff facing a claim may bring in a third party the same way a defendant may under Rule 12.1.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"13","t":"Amended pleadings","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 13.1–13.3.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"13.1","t":"Amendments","s":"A party may amend at any time before being served with a responsive pleading, or, for a pleading that permits no response and has not been set for trial, within 20 days after serving it. Otherwise amendment requires leave of court (freely given when justice so requires) or the adverse party’s written consent, and the motion for leave must attach the full amended pleading. The response to an amended pleading is due within the time remaining to answer the original or 20 days after service of the amendment, whichever is longer, unless the court orders otherwise.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"13.2","t":"Amendments to conform to the evidence","s":"Issues tried by express or implied consent are treated as if pleaded, and a conforming amendment may be sought at any time, even after judgment, though failure to amend does not affect the result. If evidence is objected to as outside the pleadings, the court may allow amendment if it aids presentation of the case, the movant shows good cause for not amending earlier, and the evidence will not prejudice the other side, considering the amendment’s effect on the outcome and the prejudice to the opposing party. Where a party is in default for nonappearance, Rules 42.4 and 67.1 apply.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"13.3","t":"Relation back","s":"An amendment relates back when the claim or defense arises from the conduct, act, omission, or event in the original pleading. An amendment changing the defendant relates back if, in addition and within the prescriptive (limitations) period, the new party knew of the pending action so as not to be prejudiced in defending on the merits and, but for a mistake about identity, would have been sued originally. Adding a plaintiff relates back if the claim arises from the same conduct and the defendant knew, within the prescriptive period, of the new plaintiffs’ claim and their involvement in the original action.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"14","t":"Joinder of claims","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 14.1–14.2.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"14.1","t":"Joinder of claims","s":"A party may join as many independent or alternative claims as it has against an opposing party.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"14.2","t":"Contingent claims","s":"A claim that depends on another claim being pursued to conclusion may be joined in the same action, but the court will not decide the contingent claim until the principal claim is resolved.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"15","t":"Standing and capacity","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 15.1–15.4 on real party in interest, minors and incapacitated persons, common names, and unknown defendants.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"15.1","t":"Real party in interest (legitimación activa)","s":"Actions are prosecuted in the name of the person holding the right under law, though a person authorized by law may sue without that person, and the Commonwealth may sue for another’s benefit where the law allows. No dismissal for that defect until, after objection, a reasonable time is allowed for the real party to ratify, join, or be substituted, which has the same effect as if that party had originally sued.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"15.2","t":"Minors and incapacitated persons","s":"A minor appears through a parent with patria potestad or, failing that, a general guardian; an adult or emancipated person adjudged incapacitated appears through a general guardian; the court may appoint a guardian ad litem (defensor judicial) when convenient or required by law. In the situations in Rule 4.4(c) and Rule 22.2, the court decides the party’s mental state and whether a guardian ad litem is needed.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"15.3","t":"Defendants under a common name","s":"Two or more persons doing business under a common name may be sued under that name, and serving one of them suffices.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"15.4","t":"Defendant of unknown name","s":"A plaintiff who does not know a defendant’s true name must say so and state the specific claim against that defendant, may use a fictitious name, and must promptly amend once the true name is discovered.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"16","t":"Indispensable joinder of parties","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 16.1–16.2.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"16.1","t":"Indispensable parties","s":"Persons with a common interest without whom the controversy cannot be adjudicated must be joined as plaintiffs or defendants; one who refuses to join as plaintiff may be made a defendant.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"16.2","t":"Necessary but not indispensable parties","s":"The court may order persons subject to its jurisdiction to appear if their joinder is needed to give complete relief to existing parties.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"17","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 17.1–17.2.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"17.1","t":"Permissive joinder","s":"Any number of persons may join as plaintiffs or be joined as defendants if the claims arise from the same act, omission, event, or series and share a common question of fact or law; each party need not be interested in all the relief, and judgment may be entered for or against parties according to their respective rights and liabilities.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"17.2","t":"Orders to prevent prejudice","s":"The court may issue orders to prevent embarrassment, delay, or expense to a party joined with another who has no claims against it, including separate trials and judgment on fewer than all claims or parties under Rule 42.3.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"18","t":"Misjoinder of parties","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped by the court at any stage on just terms, and any claim may be severed and proceed separately.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"19","t":"Interpleader","s":"Persons with justiciable claims against the plaintiff may be joined as defendants and required to litigate among themselves when the plaintiff is or may be exposed to double or multiple liability, even if their claims lack a common origin or the plaintiff denies liability in whole or part. A defendant facing similar exposure may get the same relief by crossclaim, third-party claim, or counterclaim, and this rule supplements Rule 17.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"20","t":"Class actions","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 20.1–20.7 on class actions, residual class funds, and accommodations for parties who cannot communicate effectively.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"20.1","t":"Prerequisites for a class action","s":"One or more members may sue or be sued as class representatives only if the class is so numerous that joinder is impracticable, there are common questions of law or fact, the representatives’ claims or defenses are typical, and they will fairly and adequately protect the class.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"20.2","t":"Types of class actions","s":"Beyond Rule 20.1, the action must fit one category: separate suits would risk inconsistent adjudications or would as a practical matter dispose of absent members’ interests; the opposing party acted or refused to act on grounds applicable to the whole class so that injunctive or declaratory relief is appropriate; or common questions predominate and a class action is superior, considering members’ interest in individual control, existing litigation, the desirability of the forum, and manageability.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"20.3","t":"Certification order; notice; judgment","s":"As soon as practicable, and after a hearing, the court decides whether the case proceeds as a class action; the order may be conditional and changed before a merits decision. In Rule 20.2(c) classes the court must direct the best practicable notice, including individual notice to identifiable members unless unduly burdensome, telling members they may opt out by a set date, that the judgment binds those who do not, and that they may appear through counsel. Judgments describe the class members bound, and actions may proceed as to particular issues or through subclasses.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"20.4","t":"Orders in class actions","s":"The court may issue orders setting the course of proceedings, requiring notice to members, imposing conditions on representatives or intervenors, striking representative allegations, setting special discovery rules and deadlines, and dealing with similar procedural matters; these may be combined with a Rule 37 order and modified over time.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"20.5","t":"Dismissal or settlement","s":"A class action may not be dismissed or settled without court approval, and all members must be notified of the proposed dismissal or settlement as the court directs.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"20.6","t":"Residual (cy pres) class funds","s":"Every judgment or settlement order in a class action, or in a suit for unidentified beneficiaries, that sets up a claims process must provide for disbursing leftover funds. After the parties report that approved class members and court-authorized costs, fees, and expenses have been paid, the court orders all residual funds paid to the Fund for Access to Justice (Fondo para el Acceso a la Justicia, Law 165-2013) for civil, administrative, and family legal services for low-income people or purposes related to the litigation or the class’s interests.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"20.7","t":"Parties who cannot communicate effectively","s":"A party who is deaf or hard of hearing, or has another condition preventing effective communication, must be assigned a sign-language or lip-reading interpreter or other reasonable accommodation consistent with the Americans with Disabilities Act. The court must arrange it as soon as it learns of the need, reschedule promptly if a hearing must be suspended, and presume the need if it is disputed.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"21","t":"Intervention","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 21.1–21.7, including third-party claims to attached property.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"21.1","t":"Intervention as of right","s":"On timely application, anyone may intervene when a law or these rules give an unconditional right, or when the applicant claims a right or interest in the property or subject matter that may in fact be affected by the final disposition (the text does not add the federal “adequately represented” limitation).","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"21.2","t":"Permissive intervention","s":"On timely application, the court may allow intervention when a law gives a conditional right or the applicant’s claim or defense shares a question of law or fact with the main action. When a party relies on a law, executive order, or regulation administered by a government officer or agency, the court orders that party to notify the officer or agency, which may seek to intervene; the court weighs undue delay and prejudice to the original parties.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"21.3","t":"Constitutional challenges","s":"When the constitutionality of a Commonwealth law, executive order, franchise, or administrative regulation is challenged in a suit where the Commonwealth or its officer or agency is not a party, the court must notify the Secretary of Justice and allow the Commonwealth to intervene, and may order it to appear.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"21.4","t":"Intervention procedure","s":"The would-be intervenor serves the motion on all parties under Rule 67, stating its grounds and attaching a pleading setting out the claim or defense.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"21.5","t":"Third-party claims to attached property","s":"When a marshal executes an order of execution, attachment, or similar order against personal or real property and a third party claims the property or an interest in it, that third party may file a complaint in intervention governed by these rules.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"21.6","t":"Motion for delivery of property; bond","s":"An intervenor seeking possession of attached property must file a motion, and the parties get an opportunity for a hearing. If granted, the intervenor must post a bond for the amount of the attachment plus any further sum the court sets to protect the affected party.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"21.7","t":"Conditions of the bond","s":"The bond is conditioned on returning the property and answering for any deterioration or total loss, plus any other just compensation, if the claimant fails to establish the right; if the claimant prevails, the bond is canceled.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"22","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 22.1–22.4.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"22.1","t":"Death of a party","s":"If the claim is extinguished by death, judgment of dismissal is entered. If not, the parties or their counsel must notify the court and other parties of the death within 30 days after learning of it, and the court orders substitution on a motion filed within 90 days after that notice; the motion may be filed by successors or representatives and is served on parties under Rule 67 and on nonparties under Rule 4, and the complaint is amended only to reflect the substitution. If no substitution is sought in time, the action is dismissed without prejudice; if the right survives to or against remaining parties, the case continues as to them.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"22.2","t":"Incapacity","s":"If a party becomes incapacitated, the court may, on motion served as in Rule 22.1, allow the action to continue by or against the party’s guardian or guardian ad litem.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"22.3","t":"Transfer of interest","s":"After a transfer of interest the action may continue by or against the original party unless the court, on motion served as in Rule 22.1, orders the transferee substituted or joined.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"22.4","t":"Public officers","s":"When a federal, Commonwealth, municipal, or agency officer sued in an official capacity dies, resigns, or otherwise leaves office during the case, the action is not dismissed and the successor is automatically substituted.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"23","t":"General provisions on discovery","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 23.1–23.4. Instead of federal Rule 26(a) initial disclosures, Rule 37.1 requires counsel to exchange documents, witness information, and expert information at a case-management meeting; the request-based methods carry their own response times (30 days for interrogatories, 15 days for document requests, 20 days for admissions).","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"23.1","t":"Scope of discovery; experts; preservation; supplementation","s":"Unless limited by the court, parties may discover any nonprivileged matter relevant to the subject matter of the action, including ESI, documents, and witnesses’ identities; inadmissibility is no objection if the request is reasonably likely to lead to admissible evidence (a broader “subject matter” standard than federal proportionality). Trial-preparation materials are discoverable, but not counsel’s or a representative’s mental impressions, conclusions, opinions, or legal theories; a party may demand a list of trial witnesses with a brief summary of each one’s testimony and copies of all witness statements, and anyone may get a copy of their own prior statement. For experts, interrogatories may obtain names and addresses of consulted and testifying experts and, for testifying experts, the subject, a summary of opinions, and their basis; non-testifying retained experts are discoverable only on exceptional circumstances or under Rule 32.2; the seeking party pays reasonable expert fees (subject to an inability-to-pay showing), and the court may call its own experts. Anyone on notice of a possible claim must preserve evidence (ESI subject to Rule 34.3), and responding parties have a continuing duty to update or correct answers that they know are incomplete or incorrect; breaching preservation draws sanctions including contempt, and failure to supplement results in exclusion of the unsupplemented evidence at trial, though the requesting party may still use it.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"23.2","t":"Limits and protective orders","s":"On its own or on motion, the court may limit discovery that is duplicative or cumulative, obtainable more conveniently or cheaply, already obtainable by the requester, or costlier than its benefit. On motion with a certification of a good-faith attempt to resolve the dispute under Rule 34.1 and on good cause, the court may protect a party or person from harassment, oppression, or undue burden or expense, including by barring discovery, setting terms, changing the method, limiting subjects, restricting attendance, sealing depositions, protecting trade secrets, or ordering sealed simultaneous filings. If protection is denied, the court may order discovery to proceed, and Rule 34 governs expenses and fees.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"23.3","t":"Privilege claims; inadvertent production","s":"A party withholding information as privileged or trial-preparation material must make the claim expressly and describe what is withheld so others can assess it without disclosure. If privileged information is produced inadvertently, the producing party notifies the receiver, who must promptly return, sequester, or destroy it and all copies, may not use or disclose it until the claim is resolved, may submit it to the court under seal, and must take reasonable steps to retrieve anything already disclosed; the producing party must preserve it until resolution.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"23.4","t":"Sequence of discovery","s":"Discovery methods may be used in any order, and one party’s discovery does not delay another’s unless the court orders otherwise on motion.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"24","t":"Depositions before action or pending appeal","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 24.1–24.2.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"24.1","t":"Before an action is filed","s":"A person expecting to be a party to an action who cannot yet bring it may file a sworn petition to perpetuate testimony, stating the expected action, the facts to be established and reasons, the expected adverse parties, the deponents and substance of testimony, and whether the deposition will be oral or written. The petition is served on expected adverse parties as a summons is served under Rule 4.4, giving at least 15 days to appear and oppose (with publication or other means allowed if they cannot be found); if satisfied that perpetuation may prevent a failure or delay of justice, the court issues an order and the deposition may be used in a later action under Rule 29.1.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"24.2","t":"Pending appeal","s":"While an appeal is pending or the time to appeal has not expired, the trial court may allow depositions to perpetuate testimony for further proceedings, on a motion naming the deponents, the substance of expected testimony, and the reasons; it may grant leave if needed to prevent a failure or delay of justice.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"25","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 25.1–25.3.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"25.1","t":"Within Puerto Rico","s":"Depositions in Puerto Rico are taken before a person authorized to administer oaths under Puerto Rico law or a person the court designates, who may administer oaths, take testimony, and direct the deposition; the oath-giver need not remain after swearing the deponent. For a litigant recognized as indigent, the court may order the deposition under conditions it deems convenient.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"25.2","t":"Outside Puerto Rico","s":"Depositions outside Puerto Rico, on notice, are taken before a person authorized to administer oaths in Puerto Rico or the place of the deposition, before a person appointed by commission, or by letter rogatory (addressed “To the Appropriate Judicial Authority in” the place). Evidence from a letter rogatory should not be excluded merely because it is not verbatim, not under oath, or otherwise not in Puerto Rico form.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"25.3","t":"Disqualification for interest","s":"No deposition may be taken before a relative within the fourth degree of consanguinity or second of affinity, employee, or attorney of a party, a relative or employee of such attorney, or a person financially interested in the action, except as Rule 26 allows.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"26","t":"Stipulations about depositions and discovery","s":"If not contrary to the Rule 37.3 scheduling order, parties may stipulate that depositions be taken before any person, at any time or place, on any notice, and in any manner and used like other depositions; modify procedures for other discovery methods; and let any attorney present swear the deponent, reporter, and translator.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions by oral examination","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 27.1–27.9 on timing, notice, recording, remote depositions, entity depositions, objections, review and signature, and certification.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"27.1","t":"When depositions may be taken","s":"After the action begins any party may depose any person, including a party, without leave, except that the plaintiff needs leave to take a deposition within 30 days after service on the defendant, unless the defendant has begun discovery or the notice certifies the deponent is about to leave Puerto Rico and will be unavailable (counsel’s signature certifies those facts and is subject to Rule 9). Witnesses are compelled by subpoena under Rule 40, and a prisoner may be deposed only with prior leave.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"27.2","t":"Notice; time, place, and method","s":"A party must give at least 20 days’ written notice to all other parties, stating date, time, place, method, and each deponent’s name and address (or a description). A notice to a party may include a document request under Rule 31; documents subpoenaed from a nonparty must be listed in the notice, and Rule 40.4 governs the place of examination and subpoenas.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"27.3","t":"Recording method","s":"Depositions may be recorded stenographically or by any audio-video or digital method that preserves the integrity of the proceeding and allows reproduction. If a party or deponent is deaf or cannot otherwise communicate effectively, the deposition must be video- or digitally recorded to preserve sign-language or lip-reading testimony, at the noticing party’s expense.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"27.4","t":"Remote depositions","s":"By written stipulation, or on motion and court order, an oral deposition may be taken by telephone, videoconference, or other remote means, on at least 20 days’ written notice to the deponent and all parties stating date, time, place, and method. Requested documents must be provided reasonably in advance and shared with the reporter and other parties; if the noticing party appears remotely it must arrange for others to do so too, and any party may appear remotely if the noticing party attends in person. The deposition takes place where the deponent answers before the authorized officer.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"27.5","t":"Court regulation","s":"On motion of a noticed party and for good cause, the court may lengthen or shorten the time for the deposition and regulate its date, place, order, and other Rule 27.2–27.3 matters.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"27.6","t":"Depositions of organizations","s":"A notice or subpoena may name a private corporation, partnership, or association and describe the examination topics with reasonable particularity; absent a designation by the requesting party, the organization designates the persons with the most knowledge to testify on its behalf and may specify their topics. A nonparty organization’s subpoena must advise it of this duty, and the rule also applies to the Government of Puerto Rico, its instrumentalities, public corporations, and municipalities.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"27.7","t":"Examination, oath, objections, and conduct","s":"Examination and cross-examination proceed under oath before the Rule 25 officer, and every deposition must be transcribed unless all parties stipulate otherwise; the noticing party pays the reporter, transcript, and the deponent’s copy. Only objections based on privilege or that would be waived if not made may be stated and argued; others are noted and the answer given subject to them (but in depositions to perpetuate testimony, needed objections must be made so questions can be rephrased). Objections must be concise and non-suggestive, and a deponent must answer except to preserve a privilege, enforce a court-ordered limit, or when a question is clearly improper and would cause great harm; counsel may instruct not to answer only to preserve privilege or enforce a court order, must state the grounds on the record, may call the presiding judge by telephone for a ruling, may not interrupt to confer with the deponent or stop the record without all parties’ stipulation, and a party may instead send sealed written questions.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"27.8","t":"Review, correction, and signature","s":"The reporter delivers the transcript to the noticing party within 45 days after the deposition ends; the deponent then gets it for review within 10 days unless review is waived on the record, and any changes in form or substance must be submitted within 30 days after notice of the transcript. For untranscribed recorded depositions, the deponent reviews within 30 days after delivery and submits changes in that time. Changes and reasons are noted, the deponent signs unless signature is waived or the deponent is ill, unavailable, or refuses (with the reason noted), and the deposition may then be used unless the court suppresses it under Rule 29.3.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"27.9","t":"Certification and custody","s":"The officer (or recorder) certifies the oath and accuracy, seals and labels the original, and delivers it to the noticing party within the Rule 27.8 time; that party must notify all others, keep the original, and produce it at trial. Exhibits are marked and attached (copies may be substituted after verification), copies are available to any party or the deponent for a fee, and where transcription is waived the noticing party must preserve and produce the original recording at trial.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"28","t":"Depositions on written questions","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 28.1–28.3.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"28.1","t":"Notice and service of questions","s":"The noticing party serves its questions with a notice naming the deponent, the officer, and the recorder. Cross-questions are due within 10 days after the notice, redirect questions within 5 days after cross-questions, and recross questions within 3 days after redirect questions.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"28.2","t":"Taking responses; duties","s":"The noticing party gives the notice and questions to the officer, who promptly takes the testimony and prepares, certifies, and handles the deposition under Rules 27.7–27.9; the noticing party must notify others when it receives the deposition, keep the original, and produce it at trial.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"28.3","t":"Protective orders","s":"After questions are served and before testimony is taken, on prompt motion, notice, and good cause, the court may enter any Rule 23.2 order, change the officer, or require that the deposition be taken only by oral examination.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"29","t":"Use of depositions in court","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 29.1–29.3.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"29.1","t":"Use of depositions","s":"At trial or a hearing, a deposition may be used against any party present, represented, or duly notified, to the extent admissible under the Rules of Evidence as if the deponent were testifying: by any party to contradict or impeach; by an adverse party for any purpose if the deponent is a party or a party organization’s officer, director, managing agent, or Rule 27.6 designee; and by any party for any purpose if the witness is dead, outside Puerto Rico and burdensome to bring (unless the offering party procured the absence), unable to attend due to age, illness, or infirmity, unable to be subpoenaed, or in exceptional circumstances on motion. An adverse party may require introduction of other relevant parts, substitution of parties does not affect use, and depositions from a dismissed earlier action (in Puerto Rico or a U.S. court) involving the same issue and parties may be used in the new action.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"29.2","t":"Objections to admissibility","s":"At trial or a hearing, a party may object to any deposition testimony on any ground that would exclude it if the witness were testifying live.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"29.3","t":"Effect of errors and irregularities","s":"Notice defects are waived unless written objection is promptly served; objections to the officer’s qualification are waived unless made before the deposition starts or as soon as discoverable. Objections to competence or admissibility survive unless the ground could have been cured at the time; curable errors in the manner of taking, form of questions or answers, oath, or conduct are waived unless timely objected to; objections to the form of written questions must be served within the time for the next round and within 5 days after the last permitted questions. Errors in transcription, preparation, signing, certification, sealing, or filing are waived unless a motion to suppress is filed within 30 days after the defect was or should have been discovered.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"30","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 30.1–30.3. The text sets no numerical cap on interrogatories, unlike federal Rule 33’s 25-question limit.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"30.1","t":"Procedure","s":"Written interrogatories may be served on the plaintiff without leave after the action begins and on any other party after 30 days from that party’s service of summons (the limit does not apply if the defendant starts discovery first); entity parties answer through an officer or agent with available information. Each is answered separately, fully, in writing, and under oath unless objected to; objections are stated by motion with a copy of the interrogatory, partial objections must quote the question and grounds and be served with answers to the rest, and answers or objections are due within 30 days after service, which the court may extend or shorten for cause. The serving party may challenge answers by motion quoting the question and answer, subject to Rule 34.1, and may seek a Rule 34 order.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"30.2","t":"Scope; use at trial","s":"Interrogatories may cover any matter within Rule 23 and answers may be used as the Rules of Evidence allow; an interrogatory is not objectionable merely because it calls for an opinion or contention about facts or the application of law, though the court may defer or excuse the answer for good cause.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"30.3","t":"Option to produce business records or ESI","s":"When the answer can be found in the answering party’s books, documents, records, or ESI and the burden is substantially the same for both sides, it is enough to specify the records and give a reasonable opportunity to examine, audit, and copy them.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"31","t":"Production of documents and things; entry on land","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 31.1–31.2.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"31.1","t":"Scope","s":"Subject to Rule 23.2, a party may request another party to produce for inspection, copying, or photographing nonprivileged documents, ESI (translated into usable form if necessary), books, photographs, and tangible things within Rule 23.1 in its possession, custody, or control, or to permit entry onto land or property to inspect, measure, survey, or photograph. The request specifies the date, time, place, and manner and may set just terms.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"31.2","t":"Procedure; 15-day response","s":"Requests may be served on the plaintiff without leave after commencement and on any other party after 30 days from its service of summons (unless the defendant started discovery first), describing items with reasonable particularity. The responding party must respond within 15 days, stating for each item that inspection will be permitted unless objected to, with reasons; the requester may seek a Rule 34.2 order. Documents must be produced as kept in the usual course of business, organized to match the requests, and ESI in the form ordinarily maintained or a reasonably usable form, in only one form.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"32","t":"Physical and mental examinations","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 32.1–32.2.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"32.1","t":"Order for examination","s":"When the physical or mental condition (including blood group or genetic makeup) of a party or person in its custody or care is in controversy, the court may on motion and notice order an examination by a licensed professional, specifying the date, time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and examiner. A party who makes allegations about its own physical or mental condition waives privacy in the related medical or psychological records.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"32.2","t":"Examiner’s report","s":"The party that obtained the examination must give the examined party a detailed written report within 45 days after the exam, and is then entitled to reports of other exams of the same condition; the court may compel delivery, and may exclude the examiner’s testimony if the examiner fails or refuses to report. By obtaining the report or deposing the examiner, the examined party waives privilege as to anyone else who has examined or may examine the same condition, in this or any action on the same controversy.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"33","t":"Requests for admission","s":"A party may serve written requests to admit the truth of matters within Rule 23.1 (facts, opinions of fact, application of law to fact, and genuineness of documents), on the plaintiff without leave after commencement and on others after 30 days from their service (unless the defendant started discovery first). Each matter is deemed admitted unless within 20 days after service (or as the court allows) the party serves a sworn answer or written objection; a defendant need not respond before 20 days after service of the summons and complaint, and the request must warn the defendant that failure to answer means admission. Answers must specifically deny or explain in detail why the party cannot admit or deny, lack of information is not an excuse without a showing of reasonable inquiry, and justiciable controversy alone is not an objection; the court may rule on sufficiency, deem matters admitted, or order amended answers, with Rule 34.2(c) expenses. Admissions are conclusive for the pending action only unless the court permits withdrawal or amendment, which it may allow if that aids a merits decision and the requesting party does not show prejudice.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"34","t":"Discovery disputes and sanctions","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 34.1–34.6 on meet-and-confer, motions to compel, sanctions, and expenses.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"34.1","t":"Meet-and-confer certification","s":"The court will consider a discovery motion only if it contains the movant’s particularized certification that it made prompt, reasonable, good-faith efforts to reach agreement with opposing counsel on the issues in the motion and those efforts failed.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"34.2","t":"Motion to compel","s":"After good-faith efforts fail, a party may move to compel answers when a deponent refuses to answer under Rules 27 or 28, an organization fails to designate under Rule 27.6, a party fails to answer Rule 30 interrogatories, or a party fails to respond to or allow a Rule 31 inspection; an oral deposition may be completed or adjourned before moving. Evasive or incomplete answers count as failures to answer. After a hearing opportunity, the losing side (party, deponent, or advising attorney) pays the winner’s reasonable expenses, including attorney’s fees, unless the position was substantially justified or an award would be unjust, and expenses may be apportioned.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"34.3","t":"Failure to obey an order; ESI loss","s":"A deponent’s refusal to be sworn or to answer after a court order may be treated as contempt. When a party, officer, managing agent, or designee disobeys a discovery order (including under Rules 32 and 34.2), the court may deem facts established, bar claims, defenses, or evidence, strike pleadings, stay proceedings, dismiss, enter default judgment, hold the party in contempt (except for an order to submit to a physical or mental exam), or impose monetary sanctions on any party, witness, or attorney, and must award expenses including attorney’s fees unless justified or unjust. A party shown with reliable evidence to have destroyed evidence or breached its preservation duty is subject to sanctions, but no sanction may be imposed for ESI lost through routine, good-faith operation of an electronic system unless the party had been asked to preserve it beforehand, and the party bears the burden of proving that cause.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"34.4","t":"Expenses for failure to admit","s":"If a party fails to admit a document’s genuineness or a matter’s truth under Rule 33 and the requester later proves it, the court awards the reasonable cost of that proof, including attorney’s fees, unless the request was objectionable, the admission had no substantial importance, the party reasonably believed it would prevail, or there was another good reason.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"34.5","t":"Failure to attend or respond","s":"If a party, officer, managing agent, or designee fails to appear for a properly noticed deposition, to serve answers or objections to interrogatories, or to respond in writing to an inspection request, the court may enter any Rule 34.3(b)(1)–(3) order and must award reasonable expenses including attorney’s fees unless justified or unjust. That the discovery was objectionable is no excuse unless a Rule 23.2 protective order was obtained. A party who notices a deposition and fails to attend, or fails to subpoena a witness who then does not appear, may be ordered to pay the expenses and fees of parties who attended.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"34.6","t":"Expenses against the Commonwealth","s":"Expenses and attorney’s fees under Rule 34 may be imposed on the Commonwealth only after a prior hearing.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"35","t":"Offers of judgment and payment","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 35.1–35.4.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"35.1","t":"Offer of judgment","s":"A party defending a claim may, at any time more than 20 days before trial begins, serve an offer to allow judgment for a specified amount, property, or result plus costs accrued to date; the offer must be in writing, sent by certified mail, identify offeror and offeree, state any damages amount, the total amount or property and conditions, and the accrued costs. If accepted in writing within 10 days after service, either party files it and the Clerk enters judgment; otherwise it is deemed withdrawn and inadmissible except to fix costs, expenses, and fees or to enforce a judgment based on it. If the offeree’s final judgment is equal or less favorable, the offeree must pay the costs, expenses, and attorney’s fees incurred after the offer (broader than federal Rule 68, which shifts only costs); successive offers are allowed, and after liability is adjudicated an offer may be made a reasonable time, at least 20 days, before the damages hearing.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"35.2","t":"Offer of payment","s":"In a suit solely to collect money, if the defendant alleges in its answer that before suit it offered the plaintiff the full amount owed and immediately deposits it in court, and that proves true, the plaintiff recovers no costs and must pay the defendant’s costs, expenses, and attorney’s fees.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"35.3","t":"Deposit in court","s":"Where relief sought includes payment of money or delivery of a thing, a party may, on notice and with leave of court, deposit all or part of it with the Clerk, to be withdrawn by court order.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"35.4","t":"Judgment by consent (confession of judgment)","s":"Judgment may be entered without trial or even without a filed action on the consent of a person with legal capacity, for money owed or to become due or to secure another against contingent liability; once the court approves it, the Clerk records and notifies it and it is final and firm from recording. The consent must be in a sworn writing by the defendant authorizing judgment for a specific sum and concisely stating the facts and origin of the debt (showing the sum is justly owed) or the facts of the contingent liability (showing the sum does not exceed it).","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"36","t":"Summary judgment","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 36.1–36.7. Puerto Rico’s summary judgment rule is far more prescriptive than federal Rule 56: it sets filing windows tied to service and the discovery cutoff, requires numbered statements of uncontested and contested facts with pinpoint citations, a 20-day opposition, and mandatory findings when the motion does not end the case.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"36.1","t":"By a claimant","s":"A party seeking relief may move at any time after 20 days from service on the defendant (or after the opponent serves a summary judgment motion) but no later than 30 days after the court’s discovery cutoff date, on affidavits or other evidence showing there is no substantial controversy of essential and pertinent facts, for judgment on all or part of the claim.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"36.2","t":"By a defending party","s":"A party against whom a claim is made may move from the date it was served but no later than 30 days after the court’s discovery cutoff, on affidavits or other evidence showing no substantial controversy of essential and pertinent facts.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"36.3","t":"Motion, opposition, and decision","s":"The motion must contain a brief statement of the pleadings, the issues, the claim or part at issue, a concise, organized list in numbered paragraphs of all essential and pertinent undisputed facts with pinpoint citations to affidavits or other admissible evidence in the record, legal argument, and the relief sought. The opposition is due within 20 days after service and must address items (1)–(3), respond by reference to the movant’s numbered paragraphs identifying facts genuinely disputed in good faith with pinpoint citations, list undisputed facts with citations, and argue the law; the opponent cannot rest on its pleadings and must answer as specifically as the movant, or judgment will be entered if proper. Properly cited facts not properly controverted may be deemed admitted, the court need not consider unnumbered or uncited facts or uncited parts of the record, judgment is entered at once if there is no real substantial controversy of material fact and the law favors the movant, interlocutory summary judgment may resolve separable controversies, and if no opposition is filed in time the motion is deemed submitted.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"36.4","t":"Case not fully decided on motion","s":"If the motion does not dispose of the whole case, grants less than all relief, or is denied and trial is needed, the court must resolve it by findings specifying the essential facts without substantial controversy, the facts genuinely in dispute, and the extent to which damages are undisputed, and must order further proceedings, including an evidentiary hearing limited to the disputed issues; facts so specified are deemed established at trial.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"36.5","t":"Form of affidavits","s":"Supporting and opposing affidavits must be made on personal knowledge, set out admissible facts, and affirmatively show the affiant’s competence, with sworn or certified copies of referenced documents attached or served; the court may allow supplementation or rebuttal by depositions or further affidavits.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"36.6","t":"When affidavits are unavailable","s":"If the opponent shows by affidavit reasons why it cannot present facts essential to its opposition, the court may deny or defer the motion and allow a reasonable time to obtain affidavits, take depositions, or obtain evidence, or make another just order.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"36.7","t":"Affidavits in bad faith","s":"If an affidavit was presented in bad faith or solely for delay, the court must order the responsible party to pay the other side’s reasonable expenses, including reasonable attorney’s fees, and the party or attorney may also be held in contempt.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"37","t":"Case management","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 37.1–37.7 on the attorneys’ case-management meeting and report, initial conference, scheduling order, pretrial report and conference, settlement conference, and sanctions. Unlike the federal model, the deadlines in Rules 37.1 and 37.3 are expressly “de estricto cumplimiento” (strict compliance), enforced by mandatory monetary sanctions under Rule 37.7.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"37.1","t":"Attorneys’ case-management meeting and report","s":"In all contested cases except those under Rules 45 and 60, family-relations cases, and cases governed by special laws, counsel must meet no later than 40 days after the last answer of a defendant, last served codefendant, or third-party defendant, or expiration of the time to answer; plaintiff’s counsel coordinates the date. At the meeting counsel exchange legible copies of all documents, audiovisual material, and ESI a party may use to support its claims or defenses; in property-division cases an inventory with descriptions and estimated values; the name, address, telephone, and summary of information of every person with relevant knowledge; and the name, contact information, CV, opinion summary, and supporting theories of consulted or proposed experts (including occurrence experts), plus a deadline for expert reports. They also assess appointment of a commissioner or other auxiliary (proposing one agreed or two candidates), prepare a discovery plan with dates, evaluate alternative dispute resolution, report related cases, and discuss stipulations and settlement; the joint Case Management Report (Informe para el manejo del caso) is filed within 10 days after the meeting, the duty to supplement applies (sanctionable under Rule 23.1(e)), and these deadlines are of strict compliance subject to Rule 37.7.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"37.2","t":"Initial conference","s":"In all contested cases except Rule 60 cases and those under special laws, the court sets an initial conference no later than 60 days after the Case Management Report is filed; if all parties agree and the judge allows, it may be held by telephone, videoconference, or other simultaneous remote means. A party that fails without good cause to join a remote conference may be sanctioned under Rule 37.7, and if the conference then has to be held in court, the defaulting attorney (not the client) may be ordered personally to pay opposing counsel’s fees. Topics include jurisdiction and venue, joinder, amendments, stipulations, contested facts and law, ADR, discovery limits and the final discovery cutoff, protective orders, the deadline for dispositive motions, the Rule 6.2(c) amendment deadline, severance, related cases, property inventories, complex-litigation certification, referral to commissioners, settlement, and the trial date; the court may instead go directly to a pretrial conference or trial or order additional discovery conferences.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"37.3","t":"Scheduling order","s":"The court issues a scheduling order capturing the initial conference’s rulings and agreements, or, if no initial conference is held, adopting those in the Case Management Report. Deadlines and settings in the scheduling order are of strict compliance (estricto cumplimiento), subject to Rule 37.7 sanctions.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"37.4","t":"Attorneys’ pretrial meeting and report","s":"In cases set for a pretrial conference, counsel must meet informally at least 15 days before the conference and prepare a Preliminary Report Between Attorneys covering trial counsel’s contact information, a brief statement of facts and itemized damages, stipulations, each side’s position on disputed facts with legal basis, applicable law and anticipated legal questions with case law, uncontested and contested documentary evidence (with grounds for objection), witness lists with addresses and testimony summaries (except impeachment or rebuttal witnesses), expert lists with summaries, waived claims or defenses, pending and contemplated motions, amendments and why they were not made earlier, estimated trial days and hours, settlement, and other measures. The report is filed 10 days before the pretrial conference; absent good cause, documents, witnesses, or issues not identified are excluded at trial, and objections and defenses not specified are waived.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"37.5","t":"Pretrial conference","s":"Held at least 30 days before trial, with parties’ attendance if the court requires; it covers the items in the attorneys’ report, settlement, rulings on pending controversies including admissibility, and a trial plan. The resulting pretrial order records agreements, allowed amendments, and stipulations, limits trial to unresolved issues, and governs the case unless modified at trial to prevent manifest injustice.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"37.6","t":"Settlement conference","s":"The court may at any time order a settlement conference attended by the parties and their counsel.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"37.7","t":"Sanctions","s":"If a party or counsel fails without good cause to comply with the deadlines and settings of Rule 37 or any case-management order, the court must impose an appropriate monetary sanction on the party or attorney; the money goes to the Fund for Access to Justice under Law 165-2013.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"38","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 38.1–38.2.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"38.1","t":"Consolidation","s":"When pending actions involve common questions of law or fact, the court may order a joint hearing or trial of any or all issues, consolidate the actions, and make orders to avoid unnecessary cost or delay.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"38.2","t":"Separate trials","s":"For convenience, to avoid prejudice or unnecessary expense, or to expedite the case, the court may order separate trials of any claim, crossclaim, counterclaim, third-party claim, or separate issue, and enter judgment under Rule 42.3.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"39","t":"Voluntary and involuntary dismissal","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 39.1–39.4.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"39.1","t":"Voluntary dismissal (desistimiento)","s":"Subject to Rule 20.5, the plaintiff may dismiss without a court order by filing a notice before the adverse party serves an answer or a summary judgment motion, whichever comes first, or by a stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared. Unless stated otherwise the dismissal is without prejudice, but a notice of dismissal operates as an adjudication on the merits if the plaintiff previously dismissed an action including the same claim in Puerto Rico, federal, or any state court. Otherwise dismissal requires a court order on the terms the court sets, without prejudice unless the order says otherwise.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"39.2","t":"Involuntary dismissal; inactivity; dismissal after plaintiff’s case","s":"If the plaintiff fails to comply with the rules or a court order, the court may dismiss or strike pleadings, but for a first failure it must first warn counsel and allow a response, then sanction counsel and notify the party directly, and only after the party is warned of the consequences may it dismiss, giving a reasonable time to cure of no less than 30 days unless circumstances justify less. The court must dismiss civil cases with no action by any party for the last six months unless the inactivity is timely justified; motions to continue, reschedule, or extend do not count as action, and the court’s order gives parties and counsel 10 days after the Clerk’s notice to show cause in writing. After the plaintiff rests, the defendant may move to dismiss for lack of a right to relief without waiving its right to present evidence, and the court may find facts and enter judgment or defer; unless the order says otherwise, dismissals under this rule and any other dismissal except for lack of jurisdiction or failure to join an indispensable party operate as adjudications on the merits.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"39.3","t":"Dismissal of counterclaims, crossclaims, and third-party claims","s":"Rule 39 applies to counterclaims, crossclaims, and third-party claims; a voluntary dismissal by notice under Rule 39.1(a) must be made before a responsive pleading is served, or if none, before evidence is presented at trial.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"39.4","t":"Costs of a previously dismissed action","s":"If a plaintiff who once dismissed an action sues the same defendant again on the same claim, the court may order payment of the costs or attorney’s fees of the earlier action and stay the new action until the plaintiff complies.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"40","t":"Subpoenas (citación)","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 40.1–40.10 on the form, issuance, service, objection to, and enforcement of subpoenas.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"40.1","t":"Form","s":"A subpoena states the issuing court, sala, case title, and civil number, and commands the person or entity to appear and testify at a hearing, trial, or deposition, to produce or permit inspection of books, documents, ESI, or tangible things, or to permit inspection of premises, at a specified date, time, and place. A production command may be combined with or separate from a command to appear, and an ESI subpoena must specify the form of production.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"40.2","t":"Issuance","s":"The Clerk issues subpoenas on request, or an admitted attorney who has appeared for the party may issue them: to attend trial or a hearing in the sala where it is set; to attend a deposition only where the person resides, is employed, or personally does business; to produce materials in the judicial region of the sala where the case is pending (subject to Rule 31); or to inspect premises (subject to Rule 31).","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"40.3","t":"Service","s":"Service may be made by a marshal or any literate person at least 18 who is not a party, a party’s attorney, a relative within the fourth degree of consanguinity or second of affinity, or interested, by personal delivery or as in Rule 4.4; a subpoena to appear must include a check or money order for the statutory per diem and mileage, except when issued for the Commonwealth, its agencies, municipalities, or officers. Subpoenas must be served at least 20 days before the compliance date, except trial or hearing subpoenas; proof is by a copy with the server’s sworn statement or the marshal’s certification stating the date, manner, and recipient.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"40.4","t":"Protection of persons subject to subpoenas","s":"The requesting party or issuing attorney must take reasonable steps to avoid undue burden or expense, and the court may sanction violations, including lost income and attorney’s fees. A person commanded only to produce need not appear in person unless also commanded to testify. Within 15 days after service the person may serve a written objection to inspection, copying, the form of ESI, or inspection of premises, after which the serving party may proceed only by court order on notice, and the court must protect nonparties from significant expense; objection to a deposition subpoena must also be made within 15 days after service, while objection to a hearing or trial subpoena may be made at any time.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"40.5","t":"Duties in responding","s":"Documents must be produced as kept in the usual course of business or organized and labeled by request category; a privilege or trial-preparation claim must be express and describe the withheld material enough for the requester to contest it, and does not excuse producing the rest.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"40.6","t":"Deposition subpoenas for witnesses outside Puerto Rico","s":"Witnesses outside the jurisdiction are summoned for deposition by commission or letter rogatory to the competent judicial authority where they are, testimony is taken under Rule 25.2, and the transcript may be used in place of live testimony.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"40.7","t":"Subpoena unnecessary","s":"A person present in court or before a judicial officer may be called to testify as if subpoenaed.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"40.8","t":"Concealed witnesses","s":"If a witness hides to avoid service, the court may, on a sworn statement showing the concealment and the relevance of the testimony, order the marshal to serve the subpoena, and the marshal may enter any building or property where the witness is hiding.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"40.9","t":"Imprisoned witnesses","s":"On a sworn motion showing relevance, the court may order an imprisoned person subpoenaed and brought to testify at a trial, hearing, or deposition.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"40.10","t":"Contempt","s":"Failure without good cause to obey a properly served subpoena may be punished as contempt of court.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"41","t":"Special commissioners (masters)","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 41.1–41.5 on appointment, powers, procedure, and reports of commissioners, a term that includes arbitrators, auditors, and examiners.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"41.1","t":"Appointment and compensation","s":"The court may appoint a special commissioner (including an arbitrator, auditor, or examiner), set fees chargeable to the parties as ordered or payable from funds or property in the court’s custody. The commissioner may not withhold the report to secure payment, but may obtain a writ of execution against a party that fails to pay within the time ordered, and an unjustified refusal to pay may be sanctioned under Rule 34.3.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"41.2","t":"Reference","s":"The Supreme Court and Court of Appeals may refer matters to a commissioner in original-jurisdiction cases. In the Court of First Instance reference is the exception, limited to difficult accounting and damages computations or highly technical or specialized expert matters, and no commissioner may be appointed if a party shows it would cause unnecessary delay or unreasonable cost.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"41.3","t":"Powers","s":"The order of reference specifies the commissioner’s powers, the issues to report on or acts to perform, and a reasonable deadline for the report. Within those limits the commissioner regulates hearings, may require production of evidence, rules on admissibility unless the order says otherwise, administers oaths, examines witnesses and parties under oath, and on request makes a record of excluded evidence under the Rules of Evidence.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"41.4","t":"Proceedings","s":"The Clerk gives the commissioner the order at once, and unless otherwise ordered the commissioner notifies the parties of a first meeting to be held within 20 days after the order and must proceed with reasonable diligence; any party may ask the court to order expedition, and the commissioner may proceed in a party’s absence or postpone. Witnesses are subpoenaed under Rule 40 and face contempt and Rules 34 and 40 consequences; in accounting matters the commissioner may prescribe the form of accounts and receive statements from a certified public accountant called as a witness, requiring other proof if items are challenged.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"41.5","t":"Report","s":"The commissioner files a report on all referred matters, with findings and conclusions if required, by the date in the order, accompanied (unless otherwise ordered) by a transcript of proceedings, summary of evidence, and original exhibits, and may first circulate a draft to counsel; the Clerk notifies all parties on filing. The court accepts the commissioner’s findings of fact unless clearly erroneous; objections are served within 20 days after notice of the report (or as ordered), and on motion under Rule 67 the court may adopt, modify, or reject the report, take more evidence, or recommit it. The report has the same effect whether or not parties consented to the reference, but if parties stipulate that findings are final, only questions of law are considered.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"42","t":"Judgments and resolutions","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 42.1–42.4.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"42.1","t":"Definitions: judgment and resolution","s":"A “sentencia” (judgment) is any Court of First Instance determination that finally resolves the controversy and is appealable; a “resolución” is a ruling that ends an incident within the case. In an appellate court, a judgment is its final determination on an appeal or on a discretionary writ it has granted, or its dismissal of an appeal for any reason; a discretionary denial of the writ is a “resolución.”","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"42.2","t":"Findings of fact and conclusions of law","s":"In all actions the court states the facts proved and its conclusions of law separately and orders entry of judgment, and does the same when granting or denying preliminary injunctions; findings based on oral testimony are set aside only if clearly erroneous, with due regard for the trial court’s opportunity to judge credibility, and adopted commissioner findings count as the court’s. Findings are not required on motions under Rules 10, 36.1, and 36.2 or other motions (except under Rule 39.2), in default cases, when the parties stipulate, or when the court deems it unnecessary given the nature of the action or relief; on denial of summary judgment the court must make Rule 36.4 findings.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"42.3","t":"Judgment on multiple claims or parties","s":"With multiple claims or parties, the court may enter final judgment on fewer than all only by expressly concluding there is no reason to delay entry and expressly directing entry of judgment. That partial judgment is then final for all purposes as to what it adjudicates, and once entered and a copy of its notice is filed in the record, the deadlines of Rules 43.1, 47, 48, and 52.2 start to run as to it.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"42.4","t":"Relief granted","s":"Every judgment grants the relief to which the prevailing party is entitled even if not requested in the pleadings, but a default judgment may not differ in kind from or exceed in amount what was demanded.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"43","t":"Amended or additional findings","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 43.1–43.2.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"43.1","t":"Motion for initial or additional findings","s":"Findings are not required to preserve an appeal, but on a motion filed no later than 15 days after a copy of the notice of judgment is filed in the record, the court may make initial findings and conclusions where Rule 42.2 excused them, amend or add findings, and amend the judgment. A party seeking findings, reconsideration, or a new trial must combine them in one filing, which the court decides together, and sufficiency of the evidence may be raised later even without objection below. The motion must be served on all other parties within the same 15 days; that service deadline is expressly of strict compliance (cumplimiento estricto).","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"43.2","t":"Tolling of post-judgment deadlines","s":"The motion must state with sufficient particularity the facts the movant considers proved and rest on substantial issues about pertinent findings or material conclusions of law. A motion for amended, initial, or additional findings by any party interrupts the time to appeal for all parties, which starts again once a copy of the notice of the order granting or denying it, or of the amended judgment, is filed in the record.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"44","t":"Costs, attorney’s fees, and legal interest","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 44.1–44.3. Unlike federal practice, attorney’s fees are mandatory against a party that acted with temerity or frivolousness, and prejudgment interest is imposed for temerity.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"44.1","t":"Costs and attorney’s fees","s":"Costs go to the prevailing party at trial or on appeal or review unless a law or these rules provide otherwise, covering expenses necessarily incurred that the law requires or the court in its discretion finds should be reimbursed. The claimant files and serves a sworn (or attorney-certified) memorandum of costs within 10 days after a copy of the notice of judgment is filed in the record; any objection is due within 10 days after service of the memorandum, and the trial court’s ruling is reviewable by certiorari to the Court of Appeals, consolidated with any appeal of the judgment. A party that prevails on appeal files its memorandum of appellate costs (Court of Appeals and Supreme Court) in the trial sala within a jurisdictional (jurisdiccional) term of 10 days after the mandate is returned, including costs in both appellate courts when the trial judgment is reversed. If any party or its attorney acted with temerity or frivolousness, the court must impose attorney’s fees in its judgment, including against the Commonwealth, municipalities, and agencies unless expressly exempt by law.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"44.2","t":"Interlocutory costs and sanctions","s":"At any stage the court may impose interlocutory costs and monetary sanctions on a party or counsel for delay, inaction, abandonment, obstruction, or lack of diligence harmful to the administration of justice, payable electronically or as the Chief Justice provides; sanctions go to the Judicial Branch Special Fund (Law 235 of 1998), except that sanctions against the Commonwealth or its agencies go to the opposing party.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"44.3","t":"Legal interest","s":"Every money judgment bears post-judgment interest, including on costs and fees, at the rate set by regulation of the Financial Board of the Office of the Commissioner of Financial Institutions in effect when judgment is entered, stated in the judgment. The court must also impose prejudgment interest at that rate on a party that acted with temerity: from accrual of the cause of action in collection cases and from the filing of the complaint in damages cases, through judgment, except against the Commonwealth, municipalities, agencies, or officials in their official capacity.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"45","t":"Default (rebeldía)","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 45.1–45.5. A Puerto Rico default deems the well-pleaded allegations admitted, a Clerk’s default judgment is limited to liquidated sums, and judgments against the government in collection cases require proof of the claim.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"45.1","t":"Entry of default","s":"When a party against whom affirmative relief is sought fails to plead or otherwise defend, and this is shown by affidavit or otherwise, the Clerk enters its default; the court may also enter default as a discovery sanction under Rule 34.3(b)(3). Default means the allegations of the affirmative pleadings are deemed admitted, subject to Rule 45.2(b), and failure to enter default does not invalidate a default judgment.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"45.2","t":"Default judgment","s":"The Clerk may enter judgment for a sum certain or computable sum plus costs, on the plaintiff’s request and affidavit of the amount owed, against a defaulted defendant who is not a minor or incapacitated. In all other cases the party applies to the court; no default judgment may be entered against a minor or incapacitated person unless represented by a parent, guardian, guardian ad litem, or other appearing representative, the court must hold hearings or refer to a commissioner when it needs to take an account, fix damages, or verify allegations, and a defaulted party who has appeared must be notified of any default hearing.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"45.3","t":"Setting aside default","s":"The court may set aside an entry of default for good cause, and a default judgment under Rule 49.2.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"45.4","t":"Who may seek default","s":"Rule 45 applies to any party entitled to default, including plaintiffs, third-party plaintiffs, crossclaimants, and counterclaimants, and every default judgment is subject to the Rule 42.4 limits.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"45.5","t":"Default against the Commonwealth","s":"In any money-collection claim against the Commonwealth, its municipalities, agencies, or officials in their official capacity, no default judgment may be entered unless the claimant proves its claim or right to relief to the court’s satisfaction.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"46","t":"Notice and entry of judgments","s":"The Clerk must notify judgments as soon as possible under Supreme Court standards, filing in the record a copy of the judgment and proof of notice and entering it; entry in the Register of Actions constitutes entry of judgment. A judgment has no effect until a copy of its notice to all parties is filed in the record (archivo en autos), and the time to appeal runs from that filing date.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"47","t":"Reconsideration","s":"A party adversely affected by an order or resolution of the Court of First Instance may move for reconsideration within 15 days after notice of it, a strict-compliance (cumplimiento estricto) term; a motion to reconsider a judgment must be filed within 15 days after a copy of the notice of judgment is filed in the record, a jurisdictional (jurisdiccional) term. The motion must state with sufficient particularity the facts and law to be reconsidered and rest on substantial issues about pertinent findings or material conclusions of law; a noncompliant motion is denied and does not interrupt the time to seek review. A proper motion interrupts the time for all parties to appeal, which restarts when a copy of the notice of the resolution deciding it is filed in the record, and the motion must be served on all other parties within the same 15 days, a strict-compliance term; federal Rule 59(e) by contrast allows 28 days.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"48","t":"New trial","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 48.1–48.4.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"48.1","t":"Grounds","s":"A new trial may be ordered for all or some parties and issues when essential evidence is discovered that could not with reasonable diligence have been found and presented at trial, when a narrative statement of the evidence or a transcript cannot be prepared, or when substantial justice requires.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"48.2","t":"Time for motion","s":"A motion for new trial must be filed within 15 days after a copy of the notice of judgment is filed in the record, except that one based on newly discovered evidence may be filed before the time to appeal expires, with notice, a hearing, and a showing of due diligence, and one based on the impossibility of preparing a transcript or narrative statement may be filed within 30 days after that impossibility becomes known, which must occur within 30 days after notice of judgment. The text does not label these terms jurisdictional or strict compliance.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"48.3","t":"Affidavits","s":"Affidavits supporting a new-trial motion are served with it; the opponent has 15 days after service to serve opposing affidavits, extendable once for up to 15 more days on good cause, and the court may allow reply affidavits.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"48.4","t":"On the court’s own initiative","s":"Within 15 days after entry of judgment, the court may on its own order a new trial for any reason that would justify one on motion, stating its grounds.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"49","t":"Relief from judgments or orders","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 49.1–49.2.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"49.1","t":"Clerical errors","s":"Clerical errors and errors from oversight or omission in judgments, orders, or the record may be corrected at any time, on the court’s initiative or motion; during an appeal or certiorari they may be corrected before the record is sent up and afterward only with the appellate court’s leave.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"49.2","t":"Mistake, neglect, new evidence, fraud, and other grounds","s":"On motion and just terms, the court may relieve a party or counsel from a judgment, order, or proceeding for mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect; newly discovered essential evidence not discoverable in time for a Rule 48 motion; fraud (whether intrinsic or extrinsic), misrepresentation, or misconduct of an adverse party; voidness; satisfaction, release, reversal of an underlying judgment, or inequity of prospective application; or any other reason justifying relief. In divorce cases only fraud or voidness grounds apply. The motion must be filed within a reasonable time and never more than six months after the judgment or order is entered or the proceeding occurred, and does not affect finality or suspend the judgment; the court retains power to entertain an independent action, grant relief to a party never actually served, and set aside a judgment for fraud on the court. While an appeal or certiorari is pending, or after an appellate judgment, relief inconsistent with the appeal or mandate requires the appellate court’s leave, sought after the trial court indicates it would grant the motion filed within the deadline.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"50","t":"Harmless error","s":"No error in admitting or excluding evidence, or other error or defect by the court or a party, justifies a new trial or setting aside or altering a judgment or order unless refusing to act would be inconsistent with substantial justice; errors that do not affect substantial rights are disregarded.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"50.1","t":"Void judgment against a party unable to communicate effectively","s":"A judgment against a person who is deaf or hard of hearing, or has another condition preventing effective communication, may be declared void if the person requested and was not provided a sign-language or lip-reading interpreter or other reasonable accommodation under the Americans with Disabilities Act.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"51","t":"Execution of judgments","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 51.1–51.10 on writs of execution, specific-act and foreclosure judgments, supplementary proceedings, judicial sales, and execution expenses.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"51.1","t":"When execution is available","s":"A prevailing party may execute the judgment under Rule 51 at any time within five years after it becomes final and firm (firme); after that, execution requires leave of court on motion with notice to all parties. Any period during which execution was stayed by court order or by law is excluded from the five years.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"51.2","t":"Money judgments","s":"Money judgments and awarded costs are enforced by a writ of execution signed by the Clerk under the court’s seal, stating the judgment terms and the unpaid amount, directed to the marshal and delivered to the interested party; one writ may cover several judgments or orders in the case. The marshal must return the executed writ and any surplus to the Clerk within 15 days after the execution, including judicial sales; if one levy does not satisfy the judgment no new writ is needed, and each levy is noted on the back of a true copy, with the final one noted on the original.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"51.3","t":"Judgments for specific acts; foreclosure","s":"If a party fails to convey land, execute deeds, or perform another specific act within the time ordered, the court may have a designee do it at that party’s expense with the same effect, issue a writ of attachment on the party’s property, punish contempt, or enter a judgment divesting title and vesting it in another; a party awarded possession gets a writ of execution on request to the Clerk. An order for a judicial sale operates as a writ of possession and must say so, and the marshal must put the buyer in possession within 20 days after the sale, without prejudice to nonintervening third parties. A mortgage or lien foreclosure judgment orders recovery of the debt, interest, and costs by sale of the encumbered property, and if the property cannot be found or the sale is insufficient, the deficiency is collected from the defendant’s other property as in ordinary execution.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"51.4","t":"Supplementary proceedings","s":"A judgment creditor or assignee may examine any person, including the judgment debtor, in aid of the judgment using the deposition rules; for written-question depositions the subpoena may excuse personal attendance if sworn answers are delivered beforehand. The court may issue any just order needed for execution and to protect creditor, debtor, and third parties.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"51.5","t":"Levy on property","s":"A writ against the debtor’s property directs the marshal or court designee to satisfy the judgment, interest, and costs from it; if the debtor’s property exceeds the judgment, only the part the debtor designates is attached, provided it is amply sufficient. A writ on a Rule 60 summary-procedure judgment may not be executed on Saturdays, Sundays, holidays, or outside business hours absent a showing of compelling need.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"51.6","t":"Enforcement by and against nonparties","s":"A nonparty in whose favor an order is made may enforce it, and a nonparty bound by an order may be compelled to comply, by the same process as a party.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"51.7","t":"Judicial sales","s":"Notice of sale must be posted for two weeks in three public places in the municipality of the sale (such as city hall, the courthouse, and the tax collection office) and published twice in a newspaper of general circulation in Puerto Rico over two consecutive weeks, at least seven days apart; a copy goes by certified mail with return receipt to the debtor and counsel within 5 days after the first publication if the debtor appeared, or to the last known address if not. The notice describes the property, the judgment, and the place, date, and time; the court may shorten publication for perishable goods, must hold a hearing on request if compliance is challenged, and a sale without proper notice is void. The sale may be held after 14 days from the first publication, by public auction to the highest bidder between 9 a.m. and 5 p.m.; no more property is sold than needed, court officers may not bid, personal property must be in view and sold in lots, separately known parcels are sold separately (or as a third-party claimant requires), the debtor may set the order of sale, a defaulting bidder is liable for any loss on resale, payment is in cash or certified check unless the court orders otherwise, and the officer issues a certified auction record (title for personal property) or executes a public deed for real property before the buyer’s chosen notary at the buyer’s cost.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"51.8","t":"Buyer’s remedies if title fails; renewal of judgment","s":"If a buyer at judicial auction does not obtain title or possession after paying because of an irregularity voiding the sale or because the property was not subject to execution, the court, on notice and hearing, must either order the judgment creditors to repay what each received plus legal interest, or revive the judgment in the buyer’s favor up to the amount paid with interest at the judgment rate, with priority over the original creditor in a partial execution.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"51.9","t":"Contribution among joint debtors","s":"A jointly and severally liable judgment debtor who pays more than its share may use the judgment to recover the excess from the codebtors by filing proof of payment and a request with the Clerk within 30 days after paying; the Clerk notes it in the judgment register and issues a writ against the other debtors on request.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"51.10","t":"Execution expenses","s":"Reasonable necessary expenses of enforcing a judgment are recoverable by a sworn or attorney-certified request filed within 10 days after the judgment is fully satisfied; objections are due within 10 days after service of the request, and the ruling is reviewable by certiorari in the Court of Appeals. Without objection, the court approves the request but may strike improper items after letting the requester justify them.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"52","t":"Appeal, certiorari, certification, and other review","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 52.1–52.3. Key terms: appeal of a judgment, 30 days (jurisdictional) or 60 days when the government is a party (jurisdictional); certiorari against interlocutory orders, 30 days (strict compliance); all measured from the filing in the record of a copy of the notice (or mailing, if later).","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"52.1","t":"Review procedures; limits on interlocutory certiorari","s":"Appeal, certiorari, certification, and other review follow the applicable statute, these rules, and the Supreme Court’s rules. The Court of Appeals may issue certiorari to review an interlocutory order of the Court of First Instance only for orders under Rules 56 (provisional remedies) and 57 (injunctions) or the denial of a dispositive motion, and, by exception, for rulings on the admissibility of essential fact or expert witnesses, evidentiary privileges, entries of default, family-relations cases, cases of public interest, or any situation where waiting for appeal would cause an irremediable failure of justice; it need not give reasons for denying certiorari in these cases. Other interlocutory orders may be reviewed on appeal from the final judgment, subject to the Rule 50 harmless-error standard.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"52.2","t":"Deadlines and effects of appeal, certiorari, and certification","s":"Appeals to the Court of Appeals or Supreme Court from judgments must be filed within a jurisdictional (jurisdiccional) 30 days after a copy of the notice of judgment is filed in the record; certiorari to the Court of Appeals from final resolutions in voluntary-jurisdiction proceedings, and to the Supreme Court from Court of Appeals decisions in appeals or in voluntary-jurisdiction certiorari, is also jurisdictional at 30 days. Certiorari to the Court of Appeals from Court of First Instance orders or resolutions, and to the Supreme Court from other final Court of Appeals rulings in discretionary matters or its interlocutory resolutions, must be filed within 30 days after notice, a strict-compliance (cumplimiento estricto) term extendable only for special circumstances properly stated in the petition; if certiorari stays trial proceedings, the Court of Appeals must decide within 60 days after the parties have stated their positions. When the Commonwealth, municipalities, their officers, or instrumentalities (excluding public corporations) are parties, any aggrieved party has a jurisdictional 60 days to appeal a trial judgment or seek certiorari against a Court of Appeals decision in an appeal; and any term computed from the filing of a copy of a notice runs from the mailing date if that differs from the filing date.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"52.2(d)","t":"Certification to the Supreme Court","s":"The Supreme Court may, in its discretion and on its own or on request, bring up any matter pending in the Court of First Instance or Court of Appeals when there are conflicting Court of Appeals decisions, novel questions of law, or high-public-interest questions including substantial constitutional questions under the Puerto Rico or U.S. Constitution. It also accepts certified questions of Puerto Rico law that may be outcome-determinative and lack clear precedent from the U.S. Supreme Court, a U.S. court of appeals or district court, or the highest appellate court of any state or territory.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"52.2(e)–(h)","t":"Interruption of the time to seek review","s":"The time to appeal is interrupted by a timely motion under Rule 43.1 (findings), Rule 47 (reconsideration), or Rule 48 (new trial denied), and in the Supreme Court by a reconsideration motion in the Court of Appeals, restarting when a copy of the notice of the ruling on the motion is filed in the record. A timely reconsideration motion likewise interrupts the time to seek Supreme Court certiorari (computed from mailing if later than filing), the time for certiorari to the Court of Appeals is interrupted under Rule 47, and any interruption benefits every party in the case.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"52.3","t":"Stay of proceedings","s":"Filing a notice of appeal stays lower-court proceedings on the judgment or the part appealed unless the appellate court orders otherwise, though the trial court may proceed on matters not appealed and may order perishable property sold and the proceeds deposited. Filing a certiorari petition does not stay proceedings unless the appellate court so orders, but issuance of the writ does stay them unless the Court of Appeals orders otherwise; a certification request does not stay proceedings unless the Supreme Court orders otherwise, and Rule 57.7 governs TROs and injunctions. Absent an appellate order, the effects of a decision granting an injunction, mandamus, or order to do or desist, a support order, or a custody or visitation order are not stayed.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"53","t":"Special proceedings and extraordinary remedies","s":"Special legal proceedings and extraordinary remedies not covered by Rules 54–60 follow their governing statutes, with these rules applying where not inconsistent. A preliminary injunction where the main relief is a permanent injunction is governed exclusively by Rule 57 and applicable special laws, while an order to do or refrain as a provisional remedy securing a judgment in an action not mainly for injunction is governed by Rule 56.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"54","t":"Mandamus","s":"A writ of mandamus, peremptory or alternative, is sought by sworn petition. If the right to immediate performance is clear and no excuse for nonperformance could be given, the court may order the relief peremptorily; otherwise it orders an answer, holds a hearing as soon as practicable taking evidence if needed, and decides promptly, enforcing its orders like any others.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"55","t":"Exequatur (recognition of foreign judgments)","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 55.1–55.6.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"55.1","t":"Definition","s":"Exequatur is the judicial validation and recognition of another jurisdiction’s judgment by the courts of the forum where it is to be enforced, and may proceed ex parte or as an ordinary action.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"55.2","t":"Initial pleading","s":"The applicant files in the proper sala of the Court of First Instance either a complaint against all others affected by the judgment or an ex parte petition sworn by all affected persons; where minors’ or incapacitated persons’ interests may be affected, parents with patria potestad or guardians must be included.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"55.3","t":"Required documents","s":"The filing must attach a certified, legible, complete copy of the judgment that satisfies the Rules of Evidence, and a faithful Spanish translation if the judgment is not in Spanish or English.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"55.4","t":"Notice","s":"Besides serving affected persons under Rule 4, the applicant must serve the Family Affairs Prosecutor (Procurador de Asuntos de Familia) when minors’ or incapacitated persons’ interests may be affected, the Public Prosecutor (Ministerio Fiscal) when validation is sought for recording in the Property Registry under Article 45 of the Mortgage and Property Registry Law (30 LPRA sec. 2208), and the Secretary of Justice when the court finds a matter of the highest public interest.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"55.5","t":"Standards for recognition","s":"After resolving procedural issues, the court recognizes a judgment of a U.S. state or territory if rendered by a court with personal and subject-matter jurisdiction, with due process, and not procured by fraud. A judgment from any other jurisdiction additionally must come from a competent court, observe basic due process, arise from an impartial system without bias against foreigners, and not be contrary to public order or basic principles of justice.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"55.6","t":"Execution","s":"A recognized foreign judgment is executed under the procedures that apply to Puerto Rico judgments.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"56","t":"Provisional remedies","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 56.1–56.8 on remedies to secure the effectiveness of a judgment (attachment, garnishment, prohibition against alienation, replevin, receivership, and orders to do or refrain).","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"56.1","t":"General principles","s":"Before or after judgment, on a claimant’s motion, the court may issue any provisional order needed to secure the effectiveness of the judgment, including attachment (embargo), garnishment of funds held by a third party, prohibition against alienation (prohibición de enajenar), recovery and delivery of personal property, receivership, an order to do or refrain from specific acts, or any other appropriate measure, weighing all parties’ interests as substantial justice requires.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"56.2","t":"Notice and hearing","s":"No provisional remedy may be granted, modified, or vacated without notice to the adverse party and a hearing, except as Rules 56.4 and 56.5 allow. If sought before the defendant is served, the movant must serve the order setting the hearing along with the pleadings, the motion, and supporting documents.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"56.3","t":"Bond","s":"A provisional remedy may be granted without bond if public or private documents signed before a person authorized to administer oaths show the obligation is legally enforceable; if the movant is an indigent litigant expressly exempt by law from filing fees, the complaint shows an evident or demonstrable likelihood of success, and after a hearing there is well-founded fear that without the remedy the judgment would be academic for lack of assets; or if the remedy is sought after judgment (the court may then exclude specific assets). Otherwise the bond must be sufficient to cover all damages caused by the measure; a defendant may keep attached personal property, or dissolve an attachment, by posting a bond the court fixes. A surety submits to the court’s jurisdiction, irrevocably appoints the Clerk as its agent for service, and its liability may be enforced by motion without a separate suit.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"56.4","t":"Attachment or prohibition against alienation","s":"If Rule 56.3 is satisfied, the court must issue an attachment or prohibition against alienation on motion, but not without prior notice and hearing unless the claimant shows a prior property interest in the thing, extraordinary circumstances, or likely success through reliable documentary evidence that the debt is liquid, due, and payable. An affected party may move at any time to modify or vacate an ex parte order, to be heard at the earliest possible date with priority over all other matters on two days’ notice (or shorter as the court sets). Real-property measures are effected by annotation in the Property Registry with notice to the defendant, personal property is deposited with the court or a designee at the claimant’s risk, fungibles may be sold at auction, and a requested depositary must provide contact information and report changes immediately.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"56.5","t":"Order to do or refrain","s":"No order to do or refrain from a specific act may issue without notice to the adverse party unless sworn specific facts clearly show irreparable harm before a hearing can be held, or extraordinary circumstances, or likely success through reliable documentary evidence. An ex parte order is effective on notification, and any affected party may move at any time to modify or vacate it, to be heard at the earliest date and never later than 5 days after the motion is filed, with priority over all other matters and on two days’ notice (or shorter as set).","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"56.6","t":"Receivers","s":"A receiver may be appointed only if no other provisional remedy would be effective, acts under the rules for judicial administration of estates unless otherwise ordered, may not be a party, a party’s attorney, or an interested person without the affected parties’ written consent on file, and may be required to post a bond before acting.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"56.7","t":"Cancellation of a recorded attachment","s":"The court where the action is pending may order cancellation of a preventive annotation of attachment after a hearing and on posting of a bond in a reasonable amount, considering the plaintiff’s likelihood of success, the property’s value, and other circumstances.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"56.8","t":"Enforcement","s":"Orders under Rule 56 may be enforced through the court’s civil contempt power.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"57","t":"Injunctions","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 57.1–57.7 on temporary restraining orders (entredicho provisional), preliminary and permanent injunctions, bonds, labor disputes, and injunctions pending review.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"57.1","t":"Temporary restraining order","s":"A TRO may issue without notice only if a sworn affidavit or verified complaint clearly shows immediate and irreparable injury before the adverse party can be heard, and the applicant or counsel certifies in writing any efforts made to give notice and why notice should not be required. An ex parte TRO must state its date and hour of issuance, be filed and entered at once, define the injury and why it is irreparable and why notice was dispensed with, and expire on its own terms within a period the court sets of no more than 10 days after entry, unless extended for proven good cause within that period for an equal period, or longer with the restrained party’s consent, with the reasons on the record. The preliminary injunction motion must then be heard at the earliest possible date with priority over all but older matters of the same kind, the TRO is dissolved if the applicant does not proceed, and the adverse party may move to dissolve or modify on two days’ notice (or shorter as the court sets).","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"57.2","t":"Preliminary injunction","s":"No preliminary injunction may issue without prior notice, served like a summons under Rule 4.4 by delivering the order with a copy of the petition, which has the same effect as service of summons, with proof and amendment under Rules 4.7 and 4.8. Before or after the hearing begins, the court may consolidate the trial on the merits with it; even without consolidation, evidence admitted at the hearing that would be admissible at trial becomes part of the record and need not be repeated, and the court must issue an order specifying the facts found and ordering further proceedings.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"57.3","t":"Factors for a TRO or preliminary injunction","s":"The court must consider, among others, the nature of the harm to the petitioner, irreparability or lack of an adequate remedy at law, the movant’s likelihood of prevailing, the likelihood the case will become moot, the impact of the relief on the public interest, and the petitioner’s diligence and good faith.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"57.4","t":"Bond","s":"No TRO or preliminary injunction issues without a bond in the amount the court deems just for costs and damages of a party wrongfully restrained; no bond is required of the Commonwealth, its municipalities, agencies, or officials in their official capacity. The surety submits to the court’s jurisdiction, appoints the Clerk as agent for service, and may be held liable on motion without a separate suit.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"57.5","t":"Form and scope of injunctive orders","s":"Every TRO or preliminary or permanent injunction must state the reasons for issuance, be specific in terms, and describe the restrained acts in reasonable detail without referring to the complaint or other documents. It binds only the parties, their officers, agents, servants, employees, and attorneys, and persons in active concert or participation with them who receive notice of the order by any means.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"57.6","t":"Labor disputes","s":"Rule 57 does not modify Law No. 50 of August 4, 1947 (29 LPRA secs. 101–107) on restraining orders and injunctions in cases arising from labor disputes, or any other Puerto Rico law on injunctions in suits affecting employers and employees.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"57.7","t":"Injunction pending appeal or certiorari","s":"When an order or judgment granting, dissolving, or denying an injunction is appealed or reviewed, the trial court may in its discretion suspend, modify, restore, or grant an injunction during review on bond and other terms protecting the opposing party. The appellate court or a judge of it may also stay proceedings, act on the injunction, or preserve the status quo, but when review is sought of an order dissolving or denying a TRO, the appellate court may issue only an ex parte provisional order not exceeding 10 days unless it expressly extends it under Rule 57.1.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"58","t":"Eminent domain (expropiación forzosa)","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 58.1–58.9 on condemnation of real and personal property.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"58.1","t":"Applicability of other rules","s":"The Rules of Civil Procedure govern condemnation of personal and real property except where they conflict with Rule 58 or a special law; when the amount involved does not exceed the Rule 60 limit, the court may simplify the proceedings substantially along Rule 60 lines.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"58.2","t":"Joinder of properties","s":"The plaintiff may join one or more properties in one action, whether of the same or different owners and whether or not for the same use.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"58.3","t":"Complaint and condemnation file","s":"The complaint is captioned under Rule 8.1 but names the property as “defendant,” described generally by kind, quantity, and location, plus at least one owner of a part or interest. It must state the authority and statute, the public use, a description sufficient to identify the property, the rights and duration sought, the defendants for each property, the time requested for taking possession, and a request for an order recording title free of liens; before any compensation hearing the plaintiff must join everyone with a known or reasonably discoverable interest (owners, occupants, lessees, usufructuaries, mortgagees), may join others as “Unknown Owners,” and must name the owners shown on tax receipts or other title evidence if there is no recorded title. The complaint must attach the condemnation file (legajo): Exhibit A (property, registry data, cadastral number at the Municipal Revenue Collection Center, interested persons, the estimated just compensation, and public purpose, plus any personal property sought); a Property Registry certification issued within the 6 months before filing (if issued more than 3 months before filing, also a recent title study done within the 10 days before filing by a notary or an insured title company); a location consultation (consulta de ubicación), excused for municipal acquisitions consistent with an approved Territorial Plan; a survey plan; and an appraiser’s valuation report, together with a declaration for acquisition and delivery of possession and a draft resolution. The plaintiff gives the Clerk at least one copy of the complaint for defendants and three copies of the file, which are certified and sent to the Municipal Revenue Collection Center and the Property Registry.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"58.4","t":"Summons in condemnation","s":"The plaintiff delivers summonses to the Clerk at filing for immediate issuance, with the same effect as Rule 4 service; each must state the court, title, defendant, that the action is for condemnation, a description of that defendant’s property, the right sought, the authority, the use, and that the defendant may serve an answer on plaintiff’s counsel within 20 days after service (30 days if served by edict), with counsel’s name and address. Personal service follows Rules 4.3 and 4.4 for defendants residing in Puerto Rico or the United States, its territories, or possessions with known residence; on the server’s affidavit of diligent efforts, the court may order publication once a week for at least three successive weeks in a general-circulation newspaper, with a copy mailed with return receipt 10 days after the last publication to any known address, and unknown owners may be served by edict addressed to “Unknown Owners.” Edict service is complete on the last publication; service must be made within 90 days after filing or after the edict order, and if not, the court must sanction the plaintiff but may not dismiss; failure to notify an interested person does not defeat the court’s power to transfer title but leaves the compensation determination ineffective and open to relitigation by that person.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"58.5","t":"Appearance or answer","s":"A defendant with no objection may simply file an appearance identifying the property in which it claims an interest and then receives notice of all proceedings. A defendant with defenses or objections must serve an answer within 20 days after notice of the condemnation (30 days if served by edict) identifying the property, the nature and extent of its interest, and all defenses and objections, which are otherwise waived; at the just-compensation hearing any defendant, whether or not it appeared or answered, may present evidence on the amount and share in the award.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"58.6","t":"Amendment of pleadings","s":"The plaintiff may amend without leave until a defendant first appears; afterward leave is required, at any time before the compensation hearing, only for good cause and not if it would amount to a dismissal barred by Rule 58.8. The plaintiff need not serve a copy of the amendment but must give notice of its filing under Rule 67 to appearing parties affected and under Rule 58.4 to nonappearing parties affected, and defendants may answer it within the Rule 58.5 time.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"58.7","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"If a defendant dies, becomes incapacitated, or transfers its interest after joinder, the court may order substitution on motion and notice of hearing, served on nonparties as in Rule 58.4(c).","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"58.8","t":"Dismissal","s":"The plaintiff may dismiss as to a property by notice if no compensation hearing has begun and it has not acquired title or another interest or taken possession; by stipulation with the interested defendant before entry of judgment vesting title or possession (and the court may vacate a judgment if so stipulated); or by court order on motion and hearing any time before compensation is determined and paid, but not as to property already possessed or acquired without first awarding just compensation. Dismissals are without prejudice unless the notice, stipulation, or order says otherwise.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"58.9","t":"Deposit and distribution","s":"The plaintiff deposits any money the law requires as a condition of condemnation, and may deposit where a statute permits; court and counsel must expedite distribution and the determination and payment of just compensation. If the final award exceeds what a defendant received from the deposit, judgment enters for the deficiency against the plaintiff; if less, judgment enters against the defendant for the excess. Every request to withdraw deposited funds must be sworn and certify that the requester reviewed the condemnation file and that it reflects all persons known to have an interest (owners, occupants, lessees, usufructuaries, and mortgagees), and any transaction or interest not in the file must be reported to the court.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"59","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 59.1–59.5.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"59.1","t":"When available","s":"The Court of First Instance may declare rights, status, and other legal relations whether or not other relief is or could be sought, and a request for declaratory relief is not a ground to attack an action; the declaration may be affirmative or negative and has the effect of a final judgment. Regardless of Rule 37, the court may order a speedy hearing and give the case calendar priority.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"59.2","t":"Who may seek it; interpretation","s":"Anyone interested in a deed, will, written contract, or other contract document, or whose rights or status are affected by a statute, municipal ordinance, contract, or franchise, may seek a determination of any question of construction or validity and a declaration of resulting rights; a contract may be construed before or after breach. Executors, administrators, trustees, fiduciaries, guardians, creditors, legatees, heirs, or successors may seek declarations in the administration of trusts, foundations, decedents’ estates, or estates of minors, incapacitated, or insolvent persons to determine classes of claimants, direct fiduciaries to act or refrain, or resolve administration questions including interpretation of wills; these examples do not limit Rule 59.1 where a judgment would end the controversy or remove uncertainty.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"59.3","t":"Court’s discretion","s":"The court may decline to render or enter a declaratory judgment that would not end the uncertainty or controversy giving rise to the proceeding.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"59.4","t":"Further relief","s":"Further necessary or proper relief based on a declaratory judgment may be sought by application to a court with jurisdiction to grant it; if sufficient, the court orders any adverse party whose rights were adjudicated to show cause within a reasonable time why the relief should not be granted at once.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"59.5","t":"Parties","s":"All persons who have or claim an interest that would be affected by the declaration must be made parties, and no declaration prejudices nonparties; when the validity of a municipal ordinance or franchise is at issue, the municipality must be made a party and the Secretary of Justice notified under Rule 21.3.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"60","t":"Claims of $15,000 or less (summary procedure)","s":"For a collection suit for no more than $15,000, excluding interest, unless the complaint requests ordinary procedure, the plaintiff files a proposed notice-summons (notificación-citación) that the Clerk issues at once and must serve it with a copy of the complaint within 10 days after filing, by personal delivery under Rule 4 or by certified mail. The notice sets the trial date, which must be no later than 3 months after filing and never earlier than 15 days after notice to the defendant, and warns that the defendant must state its position at the hearing or face default judgment; the plaintiff may appear personally or through counsel, may attach a sworn statement or documents supporting the complaint, and the court decides all issues at the hearing and rules immediately. If a properly notified defendant fails to appear and owes money, no witness is needed and judgment issues under Rule 45; on a showing of a substantial defense or in the interest of justice, either party may request, or the court may order, conversion to ordinary procedure without paying the fee difference, and the procedure is unavailable unless the plaintiff knows and provides the defendant’s name and last known address at filing.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"61","t":"The court always open","s":"The court is always open, within hours the Supreme Court sets, for filing papers, issuing and returning writs, and making orders.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"62","t":"Hearings and records","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 62.1–62.3.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"62.1","t":"Hearings, chambers orders, and access to records","s":"Trials on the merits are held in open court unless the nature of the proceeding, a law, or the court provides otherwise; other acts may be done by the judge in chambers or elsewhere without the Clerk. Records made confidential by law or court order may be shown or copied only to persons with a legitimate interest or by court order for good cause, and to court officials or accredited researchers only on a showing of need and express permission. Persons with a legitimate interest (who need not apply to the court) are the parties and their heirs; the parties’ attorneys, who in custody, patria potestad, or family cases may obtain social-work or behavioral-health reports in the record without a motion, with reports prepared by Judicial Branch staff or contractors served on counsel under Rule 67, subject to protective measures; notaries whose public instruments are complemented by the judicial document or who need it to cure Property Registry defects; and anyone a party authorizes by sworn statement. Others must apply to the court showing cause.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"62.2","t":"Lost papers","s":"If a paper in the record is lost, the court may allow a copy to be filed and used in its place.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"62.3","t":"Visual record for parties unable to communicate effectively","s":"When a party who is deaf or hard of hearing or otherwise cannot communicate effectively is before the Court of First Instance, the court, in its discretion and on request, must arrange for hearings and other in-person proceedings, including preliminary ones, to be video- or digitally recorded to preserve sign-language, lip-reading, or other accommodated testimony and argument; the recording becomes part of the record.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"63","t":"Disqualification and recusal of the judge","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 63.1–63.2.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"63.1","t":"Grounds","s":"A judge must withdraw, on the judge’s own initiative or on a recusal motion, for bias or prejudice toward any person or attorney in the case or having prejudged it; a personal or financial interest in the outcome; kinship within the fourth degree of consanguinity or affinity with the prosecutor, family-affairs or minors’ prosecutor, guardian ad litem, any party, or any party’s counsel; a friendship with a party, attorney, witness, or participant that could frustrate justice; prior service as attorney or adviser to a party or its counsel on the matter, or as prosecutor in a criminal investigation on the same facts; having presided over the trial of the same case in a lower court or acted as magistrate issuing an arrest warrant or summons for probable cause in a related criminal case; participation by a person or entity that arranged a loan for the judge without the usual guarantees or conditions; prior participation as a public employee acting as attorney, adviser, or essential witness in the case; a party’s attorney being or having been within the last three years the attorney of the deciding judges; or any other cause that may reasonably cast doubt on impartiality or undermine public confidence.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"63.2","t":"Recusal motion and procedure","s":"A recusal motion must be sworn and filed before the challenged judge within 20 days after the movant learns of the ground, stating specific facts with supporting documents and affidavits; if these formalities are not met, the judge may continue with the case. If the judge agrees, a written resolution states the applicable ground under Rule 63.1(a)–(i) or the specific reason under (j), notifies the parties, and the case is reassigned; if the judge disagrees, the judge stops acting in the case and sends the record to the administrative judge to designate another judge, who must decide within 30 days after the motion is submitted. Once a judge has begun acting in a case, attorneys whose participation would require recusal may not join it.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"64","t":"Substitution of judge","s":"If a judge cannot continue because of death, illness, retirement, or another reason, another judge may act; if trial has begun or ended and the successor cannot perform the duties without a new trial on all or part of the facts or rehearing a witness, the successor may take the steps needed to decide the case.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"65","t":"The Clerk’s office (Secretaría)","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 65.1–65.4.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"65.1","t":"Office hours","s":"The Clerk’s office is open every business day during working hours except Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays, but special rule or administrative order may extend hours or open it on holidays.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"65.2","t":"Clerk’s actions","s":"The Clerk handles motions and applications for writs, entries of default, default judgments, and other matters not requiring a judge’s order, subject to the court’s power to suspend, alter, or vacate for good cause. Where a writ must issue on a judicial order, a certified copy of the order suffices with full legal effect, and the Clerk may not reject any document for failure to meet formal requirements of these rules or any regulation.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"65.3","t":"Notice of orders, resolutions, and judgments","s":"Immediately after a copy of the notice of entry of an order, resolution, or judgment is filed in the record, the Clerk must notify it the same day to all appearing parties under Rule 67, and mailing is sufficient notice; notice goes to a pro se party’s last address of record or to counsel’s address in the Supreme Court registry. Defaulted parties who appeared are notified the same way; defaulted parties served personally who never appeared receive the judgment at their last known address, or by edict if unknown. For defaulted parties served by edict who never appeared, or unknown defendants, the Clerk issues a notice of judgment by edict that the plaintiff must publish once in a newspaper of general circulation within 10 days after its notification, stating the judgment and the time to appeal, with a copy sent by certified mail with return receipt to the last known address within 10 days after publication; all terms then run from the publication date, proved by the newspaper’s affidavit. The edict must contain a title (“Notificación mediante Edicto”), sala, case number, parties, nature of the claim, date of issuance, and the time to seek review or appeal before the judgment becomes final; the Clerk records the date, manner, and recipients, and any party may accept notice by signing and dating the original.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"65.4","t":"Court seal","s":"The court’s seal is affixed to writs, summonses, subpoenas, arrest orders, and copies of record documents certified by the Clerk or another officer.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"66","t":"Register of actions","s":"The Clerk keeps a “Register of Actions, Proceedings, and Interlocutory Orders” recording every action, proceeding, and interlocutory order under the applicable regulation, with the date each document or judgment is registered.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"67","t":"Service and filing of papers","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 67.1–67.6.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"67.1","t":"When service is required","s":"Every court order and every paper filed by a party must be served on all parties on the same day the order issues or the paper is filed. Parties in default for failure to appear need not be served, except that pleadings seeking new or additional relief against them must be served as a summons is under Rule 4.4 or, failing that, Rule 4.6.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"67.2","t":"How service is made","s":"Service on a represented party is made on the attorney unless the court orders service on the party, by delivery or by mail, fax, or electronic means to a pro se party’s last address of record or counsel’s address in the Supreme Court registry; if the address is unknown, the court is so informed with a copy of the paper. Delivery means handing it to the attorney or party, leaving it with a clerk or person in charge at the office (or in a conspicuous place if no one is in charge), or, if the office is closed or there is none, leaving it at the residence with a resident at least 18 years old; service by mail is complete on mailing, and by fax or email on sending.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"67.3","t":"Certificate of service","s":"When an order must by its nature be served by a party or counsel on another person, proof of service must be filed within the time the court sets by a certification of compliance with the order’s requirements, subject to Rule 9.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"67.4","t":"Filing papers","s":"Every paper after the complaint must be filed with the court, but depositions, interrogatories, requests for admission, answers to them, and offers of judgment are not filed until needed in the proceedings or ordered filed.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"67.5","t":"How papers are filed","s":"Pleadings and papers are filed with the Clerk, though a judge may accept papers, note the date received, and immediately send them to the Clerk. After filing, counsel may also send the judge a courtesy copy of a paper needing immediate attention, by delivery to authorized chambers staff, fax, or electronic means, stating that the other side was previously advised, and must then serve the other side on the same date and in the same manner.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"67.6","t":"Electronic filing and service","s":"Once the necessary administrative measures and technology are in place, pleadings, motions, and other papers are filed electronically, and orders, resolutions, interlocutory rulings, judgments, and Clerk notices are served electronically. Electronic transmission to the address or portal the Chief Justice designates constitutes filing and also service among counsel and pro se parties, and an electronic signature satisfies the signature requirement; summonses and subpoenas may not be served electronically and must follow Rules 4 and 40, and deadlines are computed under Rule 68.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"68","t":"Time","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 68.1–68.3.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"68.1","t":"Computing time","s":"Exclude the day of the triggering act, event, or default, and include the last day unless it is a Saturday, Sunday, or legal holiday, in which case the period runs to the end of the next day that is not. The Supreme Court may suspend or extend any term by resolution for good cause; when a period is less than 7 days, intermediate Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays are excluded, and a half-day holiday counts as a full holiday.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"68.2","t":"Extending or shortening time","s":"For good cause the court may at any time, with or without motion or notice, extend or shorten a period if requested before it expires (as originally set or previously extended), or on motion after it expires permit the act if the failure was due to good cause. But it may not extend or shorten the periods under Rules 43.1, 44.1, 47, 48.2, 48.4, 49.2, and 52.2 except as those rules themselves provide.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"68.3","t":"Additional time after service by mail","s":"When a party must act within a period after service of a notice or paper and it is served by mail, 3 days are added, except for periods counted from the filing in the record of a copy of the notice of judgment. Periods counted from the filing of a copy of the notice of a judgment, resolution, or order run from the date the notice is mailed when that differs from the filing date.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"69","t":"Bonds","s":"Chapter heading for Rules 69.1–69.9, including the nonresident cost bond.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"69.1","t":"Personal sureties","s":"A personal bond must be accompanied by the surety’s sworn written statement that the surety resides in Puerto Rico, owns the real property offered, and has assets, excluding exempt property, worth twice the bond amount after all debts; multiple sureties must state that their combined liability equals that of one good surety. The statement must give the surety’s residence, describe and estimate the value of the qualifying property, list liens (recorded or not), other pending bond obligations, and other known encumbrances; posting a personal or mortgage bond authorizes the court to order its annotation as a lien in the Property Registry as under Rule 56.7.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"69.2","t":"Corporate sureties","s":"A corporation organized under Puerto Rico or any state’s law to issue bonds or guarantee legally required obligations may act as surety, alone or with others, with the same rights and liabilities as a natural-person surety, if it complies with all Puerto Rico legal requirements for such businesses.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"69.3","t":"Cash deposit in lieu of bond","s":"Whenever a bond is required, the court may order and accept a deposit of the full bond amount instead.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"69.4","t":"Mortgage bond","s":"A mortgage bond must be secured by real property whose unencumbered value exceeds the required bond by one-third, proved by a certified market appraisal.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"69.5","t":"Nonresident cost bond","s":"When the claimant resides outside Puerto Rico or is a foreign corporation, the court must require a bond of at least $1,000 to secure costs, expenses, and attorney’s fees that may be imposed, and all proceedings are stayed until it is posted; the court may require an additional bond if the first proves insufficient, with a further stay. If the bond or additional bond is not posted within 60 days after notice of the order, the court must dismiss the action. No bond is required of an indigent litigant expressly exempt by law from filing fees, a co-owner suing over Puerto Rico property when another co-owner claimant resides in Puerto Rico, or a co-owner suing to dissolve, liquidate, partition, and adjudicate property located in Puerto Rico.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"69.6","t":"When no bond is required","s":"No bond is required of the Commonwealth, its officers in their official capacity, public corporations, or municipalities; of any party in divorce, family-relations, or community-property cases unless the court orders otherwise in meritorious cases; in support claims when the court so orders; or of an indigent litigant expressly exempt by law from filing fees.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"69.7","t":"Approval of bonds","s":"Clerks, marshals, and other court officers may not accept a bond unless approved by the judge of the sala where the case is pending, except a cash deposit under Rule 69.3.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"69.8","t":"Who may not be sureties","s":"No court officer, including attorneys, may act as surety in any action or proceeding.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"69.9","t":"Cancellation of bond","s":"If a party who posted a bond prevails, the court must order the bond canceled in its final judgment.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"70","t":"Time to decide cases and motions","s":"Contested cases heard on the merits and summary judgment motions must be decided within 90 days after they are submitted; all other motions, default cases, and other matters within 30 days after submission, though either period may be reasonably extended when the nature of the matter or an extraordinary cause requires. A case is submitted when the presentation of evidence ends, extended by any period allowed for legal memoranda (or until they are filed, if earlier); a summary judgment motion is submitted when the opposition is received or the time for it expires, extended by any time allowed for reply or surreply unless filed earlier.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"71","t":"Mislabeled action or prayer","s":"A defect in naming the action or in the prayer for relief does not prevent the court from granting the relief warranted by the pleadings and evidence.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"72","t":"Cases not provided for","s":"Where these rules provide no specific procedure, the court may regulate its practice in any manner consistent with the rules and applicable law.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"73","t":"Repeal and savings clause","s":"Repeals the 1979 Rules of Civil Procedure, as amended, but keeps in force the articles of the 1933 Code of Civil Procedure that survived under former Rule 72. Articles 39–42, 44–49, 249, and 252 of the 1933 Code, Section 2 of Law No. 39 of April 20, 1942, and Articles 1 and 2 of Law No. 433 of May 14, 1951 remain provisionally in effect until repealed or modified by special law.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"},{"n":"74","t":"Effective date","s":"The rules took effect July 1, 2010.","u":"https://poderjudicial.pr/Documentos/SecretariadoConf/Reglas-de-Proc-Civil.pdf"}]});
